WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.992 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.992 --> 00:00:03.486 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.486 --> 00:00:04.980 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:12.950 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:14.900 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I'm very
interested to know

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.590 align:middle line:84%
your experiences from last
class, what your thoughts were

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.470 align:middle line:84%
on the NETP discussion and
the small group conversations

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.937 align:middle line:90%
that you had.

00:00:22.937 --> 00:00:24.770 align:middle line:84%
Did anybody find
themselves in conversations

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:25.687 align:middle line:90%
that were of interest?

00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:31.850 align:middle line:90%


00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
my group, it was

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.550 align:middle line:84%
a lot of discussion
on how they themselves

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.490 align:middle line:84%
are trying to bring about
these new innovations.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:44.030 align:middle line:84%
But a lot of it's
like teacher lashback.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.780 align:middle line:84%
And some people are having
a really hard time moving

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.520 align:middle line:84%
forwards, and then some
people are doing a good job.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.640 align:middle line:84%
And it was all of them
agreeing that they

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.393 align:middle line:84%
need to get those who are
proactive about it all together.

00:00:56.393 --> 00:00:57.810 align:middle line:84%
I also thought it
was interesting,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.480 align:middle line:84%
I kind of proposed a
question to the group

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.400 align:middle line:84%
about how they see all of
these ideologies playing

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.550 align:middle line:90%
a part in higher education.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.370 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the times, specifically
the research that you guys do,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.710 align:middle line:84%
is kind of focused
on K through 12.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.710 align:middle line:84%
But how can this really be
implemented in colleges?

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.510 align:middle line:84%
And especially at
a university like

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.440 align:middle line:84%
MIT, where you have professors
being hired not because they're

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.590 align:middle line:84%
good teachers but because
they're experts in the field.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.330 align:middle line:84%
And I think that it
just kind of prompted

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.500 align:middle line:84%
an interesting discussion
about where schools

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.430 align:middle line:84%
need to prioritize, whether
they're research institutions

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.130 align:middle line:90%
or educational facilities.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.020 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Cool.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:40.960 align:middle line:90%
Other folks?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.540 align:middle line:84%
What else did you end up talking
about with your new education

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.790 align:middle line:84%
school colleagues, or what
else was interesting to you

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.640 align:middle line:84%
about the National Education
Tech Plan discussion or anything

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.140 align:middle line:90%
else?

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:58.510 align:middle line:90%


00:01:58.510 --> 00:01:59.890 align:middle line:90%
Ty, and then Omar.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it
was interesting.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.310 align:middle line:84%
Omar and I were
in the same group.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.967 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So we'll
see if this overlaps.

00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:09.410 align:middle line:84%
But there wasn't a ton of
conversation in our group.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:13.120 align:middle line:84%
But they did ask me about my
time as a software engineer

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.843 align:middle line:84%
and how we thought
about iteration

00:02:15.843 --> 00:02:17.260 align:middle line:84%
in the software
development space.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.360 align:middle line:84%
And then they tried to
start making parallels

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.067 align:middle line:84%
to how they try to implement
it in their own schools.

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:24.400 align:middle line:84%
And we talked a little bit
about some of the differences

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.058 align:middle line:84%
and where things might be
more complex or harder.

00:02:27.058 --> 00:02:28.600 align:middle line:84%
So it was an
interesting conversation

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.680 align:middle line:84%
to try to take my
experience and reapply it.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.763 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: That's cool.

00:02:31.763 --> 00:02:33.015 align:middle line:90%
That's great.

00:02:33.015 --> 00:02:33.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.143 align:middle line:84%
So again, there wasn't
that much discussion.

00:02:36.143 --> 00:02:38.060 align:middle line:84%
You kind of went over
what I was going to say.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.950 align:middle line:84%
So I guess I'll touch on the
first part of the session, which

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.120 align:middle line:90%
is the big conversation.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.110 align:middle line:90%
I was reading the chat.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.050 align:middle line:84%
And it was pretty
cool to see everybody

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.930 align:middle line:84%
commenting on everything and
seeing a bunch of examples

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.590 align:middle line:84%
and seeing either frustrations
or revelations that they never

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:59.200 align:middle line:84%
realized about the
message of focusing

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.350 align:middle line:84%
on not the technology
itself, but what we can do,

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.090 align:middle line:84%
and then how technology
can help and what

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.770 align:middle line:90%
we're trying to do first.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.710 align:middle line:84%
And that revelation for
a lot of professionals

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.068 align:middle line:84%
was pretty cool to see
develop in the chat.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:14.860 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.960 align:middle line:84%
You get to watch human
development in real time.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.290 align:middle line:90%
People encounter where they're--

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:24.430 align:middle line:84%
I didn't follow it that
closely because I was talking.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.320 align:middle line:84%
Were there frustrations
that were--

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.440 align:middle line:84%
anything on the frustration
side that stuck out to you

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:30.285 align:middle line:90%
or seemed memorable?

00:03:30.285 --> 00:03:31.910 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Not
necessarily frustrations,

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:36.610 align:middle line:84%
but sort of like, oh, that's
why this is happening,

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.470 align:middle line:84%
or, OK, now this is
starting to make sense.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:43.150 align:middle line:84%
So it's sort of like their
frustrations were cleared up,

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:43.847 align:middle line:90%
so to speak.

00:03:43.847 --> 00:03:44.680 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Great.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.810 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:47.520 align:middle line:90%
Hi, Ramon.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.520 align:middle line:84%
You've got to come up
here and hang out with me.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.530 align:middle line:84%
That's what happens when
you get towards the end.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:51.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:52.270 align:middle line:90%
Asa.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.645 align:middle line:84%
Asa and I were in
the same group.

00:03:53.645 --> 00:03:55.495 align:middle line:90%
We had a spicy one.

00:03:55.495 --> 00:03:56.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.360 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of the
discussion was about--

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.160 align:middle line:84%
a lot of the people in
our group are the people

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.410 align:middle line:84%
designing the structures that
they were trying to implement.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.180 align:middle line:84%
And something that was
interesting was this repeated,

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.270 align:middle line:84%
oh, we design something, we
send it to the administration,

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.040 align:middle line:84%
and they're like, no, that's not
exactly what we're looking for.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:17.579 align:middle line:90%
Try again.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.970 align:middle line:84%
And it's just kind
of constantly trying

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.790 align:middle line:84%
to get something that
they're like, OK, I

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:28.910 align:middle line:84%
like this, as opposed to them
being able to effectively

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:33.470 align:middle line:84%
communicate the points
that they want included,

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:34.950 align:middle line:90%
which was very interesting.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:35.870 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Well,
part of the way I

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.745 align:middle line:84%
read our group is that
we had some people who

00:04:37.745 --> 00:04:41.120 align:middle line:84%
were kind of
middle-level leaders,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.960 align:middle line:84%
and they were trying to
convince more senior leaders

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:45.930 align:middle line:90%
to make change.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.900 align:middle line:84%
And the senior leaders
were much slower

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.370 align:middle line:84%
and less interested
in that, which

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.110 align:middle line:84%
happens in basically
every kind of institution.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.340 align:middle line:84%
When you go and
visit an institution,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.140 align:middle line:84%
one way to think of it
is it is designed exactly

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.575 align:middle line:90%
to do what it's doing right now.

00:05:01.575 --> 00:05:02.950 align:middle line:84%
It functions the
way it functions

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:04.980 align:middle line:84%
because there's some
set of actors who

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.660 align:middle line:90%
want it to function that way.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.130 align:middle line:84%
Often, those folks are people
with power and control.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.680 align:middle line:84%
And it exists the way it does
because that's what they want.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.768 align:middle line:84%
And then new people come
along-- new people live

00:05:13.768 --> 00:05:15.310 align:middle line:84%
in that environment
and they're like,

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:16.930 align:middle line:84%
well, this could be better,
and this could be better,

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:18.400 align:middle line:84%
and this could be better,
and this could be better.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.020 align:middle line:84%
And then more senior
folks are like, eh,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:21.760 align:middle line:84%
but we like the way we've
done it all the time.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.718 align:middle line:84%
And so it's not uncommon
for people coming up--

00:05:23.718 --> 00:05:25.860 align:middle line:84%
I think really
healthy organizations

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.980 align:middle line:84%
recognize, oh, we need to
be constantly improving.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.430 align:middle line:84%
And constantly improving means
throwing away and destroying

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.420 align:middle line:84%
things that I have
built over my career,

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.300 align:middle line:84%
because that's the only
way you get better.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.940 align:middle line:84%
But it's pretty
understandable that people

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:39.898 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't feel that way.

00:05:39.898 --> 00:05:42.190 align:middle line:84%
It'd be like, I've spent my
career building this thing.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.010 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we just
keep doing this thing?

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.820 align:middle line:84%
And so they were
struggling with--

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.582 align:middle line:84%
and an interesting
place where lots

00:05:48.582 --> 00:05:50.040 align:middle line:84%
of people and lots
of organizations

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.970 align:middle line:84%
find themselves is, OK, we
need to do something different,

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.520 align:middle line:84%
but the people in charge
aren't really supportive.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.790 align:middle line:90%
How do we do it anyway?

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:00.440 align:middle line:90%
Where change comes from--

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:01.000 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.920 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I mentioned this.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.180 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember if I mentioned
this particular story,

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.180 align:middle line:84%
but one of the people
that I worked with on some

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.263 align:middle line:84%
of the content that's in
Iterate is this guy Peter

00:06:09.263 --> 00:06:12.722 align:middle line:84%
Senge, who's at the
Management School at Sloan.

00:06:12.722 --> 00:06:14.680 align:middle line:84%
And a neat thing about
working with him is he's

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.840 align:middle line:84%
done a ton of stuff in
education, but lots of stuff

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.670 align:middle line:90%
in many other sectors.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.445 align:middle line:84%
And it comes up periodically,
like, man, teachers,

00:06:22.445 --> 00:06:24.070 align:middle line:84%
lots of people in
education, just don't

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.840 align:middle line:90%
feel empowered to make change.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.420 align:middle line:84%
They feel like, look, I got
hired in this particular role.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.463 align:middle line:90%
I'm a math teacher.

00:06:29.463 --> 00:06:30.380 align:middle line:90%
I'm a history teacher.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:31.463 align:middle line:90%
I'm a chemistry professor.

00:06:31.463 --> 00:06:33.628 align:middle line:84%
It's not my job to
fix the university.

00:06:33.628 --> 00:06:35.920 align:middle line:84%
And Peter once said something
to me along the lines of,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.620 align:middle line:84%
everyone in every
industry feels that way.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.920 align:middle line:84%
CEOs don't feel like they're
empowered to make change

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:42.470 align:middle line:90%
in the institution.

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:43.630 align:middle line:84%
Nobody feels like
they're empowered

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.750 align:middle line:90%
to make change in institutions.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.000 align:middle line:84%
But change still
happens all the time

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.490 align:middle line:84%
because people who don't
necessarily feel empowered still

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.670 align:middle line:84%
take it upon themselves
to try new things,

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.430 align:middle line:90%
put new things in the world.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.270 align:middle line:90%
And I don't know.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.978 align:middle line:84%
It's a story I like
to retell educators

00:06:56.978 --> 00:06:59.520 align:middle line:84%
because it's like, this is not
an education-specific problem.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.840 align:middle line:90%
This is a human problem.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:01.490 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:03.865 align:middle line:84%
I thought what Lou said, from
the University of Kentucky,

00:07:03.865 --> 00:07:06.620 align:middle line:84%
was really fascinating because
I'm actually from Kentucky.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.680 align:middle line:84%
And I worked at an
organization with her before

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.460 align:middle line:84%
called the Kentucky
Student Voice Team.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:11.780 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, really?

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:12.270 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.145 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And the way
that they impact change

00:07:14.145 --> 00:07:16.860 align:middle line:84%
is by putting students
in stakeholder positions.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:20.570 align:middle line:84%
So instead of influencing
change through ineffective ways,

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:23.250 align:middle line:84%
like administration, they put
students at the forefront.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:26.233 align:middle line:84%
So she's a really big
leader in driving,

00:07:26.233 --> 00:07:27.900 align:middle line:84%
I guess, educational
change in Kentucky.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:28.700 align:middle line:90%
So I thought it was cool.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:29.370 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: That's great.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.510 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I'm glad you
had that connection.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.010 align:middle line:90%
That's fun.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:33.890 align:middle line:84%
How did you-- so she,
at the university,

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.530 align:middle line:84%
ran the student voice
thing that high school

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:39.210 align:middle line:84%
students across Kentucky could
participate in and stuff or?

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:39.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:41.700 align:middle line:84%
So it was born out of something
called the Prichard Committee,

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.620 align:middle line:84%
but now it's an
independent organization.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.410 align:middle line:84%
And the, I guess, organization
has adults and younger students

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:50.610 align:middle line:90%
as co-peers.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.800 align:middle line:84%
They put them on the same
level of the hierarchy

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.130 align:middle line:84%
to work together to, I guess,
craft a bunch of initiatives

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:57.460 align:middle line:90%
to improve Kentucky schools.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:57.630 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:58.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.750 align:middle line:84%
There are all kinds of
cool-- education technology

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.510 align:middle line:84%
has tons of cool stuff
like that, in part

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:06.600 align:middle line:84%
because lots of young people
can get interested and excited

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.940 align:middle line:84%
by technology and so bring
them in into leadership

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:10.290 align:middle line:90%
roles, partnership roles.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:11.232 align:middle line:90%
Cities have that too.

00:08:11.232 --> 00:08:13.440 align:middle line:84%
There's a group in Boston
called the Hyde Square Task

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.370 align:middle line:84%
Force, which does all kinds
of-- they usually do more,

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.430 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, community
organizing, kind of adversarial,

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.430 align:middle line:84%
like why are you
doing this to us?

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:23.680 align:middle line:90%
Why are you doing it this way?

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.200 align:middle line:84%
They have a whole
campaign they're

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.717 align:middle line:84%
doing right now against
either Shaw's or Stop & Shop,

00:08:27.717 --> 00:08:29.550 align:middle line:84%
which is separate from
their education work.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.070 align:middle line:84%
But they've gone to a
bunch of grocery stores

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.799 align:middle line:84%
in neighborhoods around Boston
and bought the same thing

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:37.140 align:middle line:84%
in each store and
basically found

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.179 align:middle line:84%
that in some minoritized,
poverty-impacted neighborhoods,

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:42.929 align:middle line:84%
the groceries are more
expensive than they

00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:44.887 align:middle line:84%
are in Newton and Wellesley
or stuff like that.

00:08:44.887 --> 00:08:46.020 align:middle line:90%
And they're holding a--

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.640 align:middle line:84%
Stop & Shop is not
happy with these kids

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.807 align:middle line:90%
being a thorn in their side.

00:08:49.807 --> 00:08:50.307 align:middle line:90%
It's great.

00:08:50.307 --> 00:08:51.190 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:52.370 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.760 align:middle line:84%
They're pretty good at
getting press for what they do

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.110 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.660 align:middle line:84%
They'll probably have to
respond at some point.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:08:59.830 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:03.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, I enjoyed letting
you all connect with them.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.740 align:middle line:84%
And it sounds like the
meeting-- every time

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.460 align:middle line:84%
you go into breakout
rooms, it's uneven.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.085 align:middle line:84%
But hopefully it's
a good sense for you

00:09:09.085 --> 00:09:11.770 align:middle line:84%
to meet some of the people who
are in higher education, who

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:15.040 align:middle line:84%
are trying to make the changes
that we're going to talk about,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.510 align:middle line:84%
in the past few weeks and
for the rest of the semester.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.230 align:middle line:84%
So hopefully that was
a neat opportunity.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:21.970 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you take
a couple of minutes,

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:25.010 align:middle line:84%
before I launch into things,
just to chat with each other,

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:26.950 align:middle line:84%
and then we can surface
some of these ideas.

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:29.140 align:middle line:84%
But the learning
journal prompt that I

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:31.570 align:middle line:84%
had for you was to
talk about, what role

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:37.600 align:middle line:84%
does technology play in
your MIT education thus far?

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.548 align:middle line:84%
And so talk to the
person next to you.

00:09:39.548 --> 00:09:41.590 align:middle line:84%
Some of you might not be
from MIT, or some of you

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:42.760 align:middle line:84%
might be in graduate
school and so you

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.870 align:middle line:84%
have some undergraduate
connections to this as well.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:45.953 align:middle line:90%
Those things are all fine.

00:09:45.953 --> 00:09:49.210 align:middle line:90%
Technology and higher education.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:53.080 align:middle line:84%
Typically, the things that
are most useful to talk about

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:57.130 align:middle line:84%
are either systems
or tools or things

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:59.740 align:middle line:84%
that you think are
particularly helpful or useful,

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:02.470 align:middle line:84%
systems or tools or things
that are particularly

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.980 align:middle line:84%
damaging or harmful or pointless
or a waste of your time,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:10.180 align:middle line:84%
and then also anything
that has started

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.640 align:middle line:84%
to seem really familiar
to you, but actually you

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.910 align:middle line:84%
think has interesting,
unfamiliar qualities or things

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:18.592 align:middle line:84%
that feel standard and
boring, but actually they're

00:10:18.592 --> 00:10:21.050 align:middle line:84%
important, maybe in ways that
we don't want to think about.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:23.150 align:middle line:84%
So turn to the two or
three people next to you,

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.650 align:middle line:84%
talk for a few
minutes about, if you

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.040 align:middle line:84%
were to explain to someone
not from your undergraduate

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.367 align:middle line:84%
institution the role that
technology plays there,

00:10:29.367 --> 00:10:31.700 align:middle line:84%
what would be the couple of
stories that you would tell?

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:32.620 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.246 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:10:34.246 --> 00:10:35.920 align:middle line:90%
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:37.090 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:38.180 align:middle line:90%
Come on back.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.208 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You need to
learn the technology.

00:10:40.208 --> 00:10:41.500 align:middle line:90%
Because of that, they believe--

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.610 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Be great to keep
trying to hear from everybody.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.990 align:middle line:84%
So would love to have some
folks who haven't shared yet.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.480 align:middle line:84%
Would love to hear, what are
some MIT highlights or lowlights

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.760 align:middle line:90%
or distinctive parts of--

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.640 align:middle line:84%
or in your undergraduate
program, distinctive parts

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.860 align:middle line:90%
of technologies?

00:10:57.860 --> 00:10:59.300 align:middle line:90%
What do you got, Jaron?

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: My group was
discussing MIT's use of Canvas.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:09.200 align:middle line:84%
And pre-COVID, MIT had this
weird proprietary thing,

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:10.290 align:middle line:90%
where they had their own--

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.130 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: What
was that called?

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:11.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Stellar.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:12.180 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Stellar.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:12.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:13.035 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:11:13.035 --> 00:11:16.670 align:middle line:84%
So they had-- each class had
their own different Stellar

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:18.990 align:middle line:84%
website, and it wasn't
standardized at all.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.670 align:middle line:90%
And it was kind of a mess.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:24.550 align:middle line:84%
It had this weird, outdated,
1990s internet-era aesthetic

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:25.050 align:middle line:90%
to it.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.660 align:middle line:90%
It was pretty horrible.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:29.240 align:middle line:84%
And then they had this
proprietary thing,

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.400 align:middle line:84%
called the Learning
Module, which

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:35.610 align:middle line:84%
was basically Canvas, like MIT
was just trying to make Canvas.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.790 align:middle line:84%
But then during the
pandemic, I guess

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.290 align:middle line:84%
they decided to make Canvas a
much more standardized thing.

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:44.040 align:middle line:84%
And so I find that to be very
helpful for keeping everything

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.810 align:middle line:84%
organized and having
assignments done through Canvas,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.610 align:middle line:84%
as opposed to being
like, oh crap,

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.430 align:middle line:84%
what's that website
I have to go to?

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:51.870 align:middle line:90%
Search up Stellar MIT.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.850 align:middle line:90%
And it was weird.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:57.300 align:middle line:84%
But I feel like, because of
that, a lot of professors

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.060 align:middle line:84%
don't fully utilize all the
different things that Canvas

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.580 align:middle line:84%
has to offer, especially
when it comes to grades

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:03.630 align:middle line:90%
and putting grades.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:09.790 align:middle line:84%
Professors and TAs don't
care about inputting grades.

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.480 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of students,
I feel, would really

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.480 align:middle line:84%
like to see real-time
feedback on their grades

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.910 align:middle line:84%
and how that actively affects
their final grade in the class.

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:21.630 align:middle line:90%
But that just isn't a--

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:22.810 align:middle line:90%
professors don't do that.

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:22.950 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:23.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.235 align:middle line:84%
And I mean, another
thing that's unusual--

00:12:26.235 --> 00:12:28.360 align:middle line:84%
we'll talk about this more
as the semester goes on,

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.027 align:middle line:84%
but it does seem a
little weird that you

00:12:30.027 --> 00:12:33.750 align:middle line:84%
would have the exact same Canvas
template for a graduate seminar

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.930 align:middle line:84%
on chemistry synthesis and
an undergraduate seminar

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:38.740 align:middle line:90%
on Renaissance poetry.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:43.095 align:middle line:84%
And if you're studying really
different kinds of things,

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:44.470 align:middle line:84%
does it really
make sense to have

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:46.970 align:middle line:84%
the same structure for all of
those different kinds of things?

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.650 align:middle line:84%
It seems like there should
be some differences there.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.090 align:middle line:84%
What were you going
to say, Emily?

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:53.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We talked about
MITx, which is like--

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:56.180 align:middle line:84%
physics use it, and then a
lot of math classes use it.

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:59.020 align:middle line:90%
And I like how it's--

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.527 align:middle line:84%
it's sometimes
recordings of a concept,

00:13:01.527 --> 00:13:03.860 align:middle line:84%
introducing you to a concept,
or it could be in writing,

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:05.590 align:middle line:84%
but it's not as
dense as a textbook,

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:06.610 align:middle line:84%
where you're kind
of like, oh, I don't

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:07.780 align:middle line:90%
want to start reading this.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:10.940 align:middle line:84%
And then it's like, right
after, when they introduce it,

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.073 align:middle line:84%
you have check
questions, where it's

00:13:13.073 --> 00:13:14.740 align:middle line:84%
kind of checking for
your understanding.

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:16.823 align:middle line:84%
And it immediately tells
if you're right or wrong,

00:13:16.823 --> 00:13:19.330 align:middle line:84%
which is really nice, because
for math, I feel like--

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:22.023 align:middle line:84%
so when you're told, oh,
this is the right answer,

00:13:22.023 --> 00:13:23.440 align:middle line:84%
you're more focused
on the process

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:25.150 align:middle line:84%
of getting to it
rather than the answer

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.340 align:middle line:84%
itself, which is a lot
more beneficial when

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:28.090 align:middle line:90%
you're doing math.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:29.465 align:middle line:84%
You care about
the process of how

00:13:29.465 --> 00:13:31.490 align:middle line:84%
to get to that answer and
not the answer itself.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.550 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's
really, really nice

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.760 align:middle line:84%
to have that platform for
especially math classes.

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.260 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And
you could imagine,

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.430 align:middle line:84%
for all of those
autograded problems

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:44.300 align:middle line:84%
you've ever done, five years
ago, you very likely would

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.650 align:middle line:90%
have done those Psets on paper.

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:48.492 align:middle line:84%
You would have turned
the Psets into a TA.

00:13:48.492 --> 00:13:51.110 align:middle line:84%
A TA would have gotten
hundreds of them.

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.260 align:middle line:84%
It would have taken
them a week, 10 days,

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.260 align:middle line:84%
two weeks to turn around
or something like that.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:57.630 align:middle line:84%
And so, by the time it
gets handed back to you--

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.080 align:middle line:84%
you might get the
answer key sooner.

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:01.460 align:middle line:90%
By the time you get--

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:03.710 align:middle line:84%
it can be days or
weeks after doing

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:05.370 align:middle line:84%
a problem that you figure out
whether you got it right or not.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.660 align:middle line:84%
And you just forgot
how you did it

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.070 align:middle line:84%
or what you were thinking
about at the time.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.310 align:middle line:84%
So you could definitely
imagine some real advantages

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.980 align:middle line:84%
to that kind of instant--
it can change the way study

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:16.020 align:middle line:84%
groups operate too,
both for good and bad.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.380 align:middle line:84%
But you could imagine that
study groups previously

00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:20.925 align:middle line:84%
would have been like, OK,
let's all write this Pset.

00:14:20.925 --> 00:14:22.550 align:middle line:84%
They all write the
Pset and turn it in.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:24.380 align:middle line:90%
You're like, I hope that worked.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.990 align:middle line:84%
But when you have the
autograding, you can be like,

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.790 align:middle line:84%
wait, half of us did
not get this right.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.423 align:middle line:90%
Why didn't we?

00:14:31.423 --> 00:14:32.840 align:middle line:84%
What can we learn
from each other?

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:35.720 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds of ways
that that instant feedback

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:38.030 align:middle line:84%
can change the
relationships that you

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:40.550 align:middle line:90%
have with that learning.

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:41.520 align:middle line:90%
Any other thoughts?

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:42.578 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Sabrina.

00:14:42.578 --> 00:14:44.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say,
something like Notability

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:49.130 align:middle line:84%
has really transformed the way
I, for example, organize all

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:51.260 align:middle line:84%
of my classwork and
the fact that everyone

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:55.400 align:middle line:84%
uses their iPads now instead
of going to paper and pencil.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:58.315 align:middle line:84%
I think, yeah, just
the fact that we

00:14:58.315 --> 00:14:59.690 align:middle line:84%
can have all of
our lecture notes

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.065 align:middle line:84%
and then upload
it to Notability.

00:15:01.065 --> 00:15:03.810 align:middle line:84%
We can annotate it, and then do
all of our homework on here too,

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.450 align:middle line:84%
and be able to compare
screens and things like that.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:07.790 align:middle line:90%
And I think that's really--

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:10.430 align:middle line:84%
before, I only ever really
used paper and pencil.

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:12.530 align:middle line:84%
And then switching-- the
fact that MIT gave out

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:13.920 align:middle line:90%
iPads to everyone.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.900 align:middle line:84%
And then, because we have the
resources to have Notability

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:23.090 align:middle line:84%
for free and the fact that we
submit everything electronically

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:25.980 align:middle line:84%
now has kind of been a
little bit of a game changer.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:28.640 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Does Notability
have any social features?

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.610 align:middle line:84%
Do you just have your own
Notability, or are there ways--

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They've recently
added some share your notes

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:38.763 align:middle line:90%
kind of gallery search thing.

00:15:38.763 --> 00:15:39.555 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:15:39.555 --> 00:15:39.810 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's also
the good old screenshot--

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:44.250 align:middle line:90%
the iPads are usually iCloud.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.450 align:middle line:90%
You can just text it.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:46.290 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: To
the next person.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:47.350 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:47.850 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:48.780 align:middle line:90%
Goo.d

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:50.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can dual
screen on the iPad, which

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:52.510 align:middle line:90%
isn't necessarily an aspect--

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.400 align:middle line:84%
well, Notability you
can also dual screen.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:56.040 align:middle line:84%
So you pull up
someone else's work.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:57.870 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:01.350 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: There's some very
specific applications for some

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:02.590 align:middle line:90%
of those kinds of things.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:03.360 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:04.562 align:middle line:90%
Other thoughts?

00:16:04.562 --> 00:16:05.520 align:middle line:90%
What do you have, Omar?

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We had the same
thought of CAT-SOOP has been

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.945 align:middle line:84%
kind of revolutionary,
I guess, in a way that--

00:16:12.945 --> 00:16:14.320 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I've
heard of this,

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:17.170 align:middle line:84%
but you've got to tell
us what it is again.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.140 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: CAT-SOOP
is an automatic tutor

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.770 align:middle line:90%
for 6.01 problems.

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:22.170 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: For 6.01?

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It used to be an old
introductory computer science

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.580 align:middle line:84%
course that was required
for most physics majors,

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:31.680 align:middle line:84%
but they phased that
out three years ago.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:33.580 align:middle line:84%
I don't remember what
6.01 specifically was,

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:35.230 align:middle line:84%
but that's what
CAT-SOOP stands for.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.970 align:middle line:90%
It's a recursive acronym.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:41.110 align:middle line:90%
But I guess, in a way--

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:42.250 align:middle line:90%
I never got to use Stellar.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:45.430 align:middle line:84%
But in a way, that's sort
of become the new Stellar

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.320 align:middle line:84%
in the sense that each class
has their own different website

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:50.278 align:middle line:84%
and each upload
problems on there.

00:16:50.278 --> 00:16:52.570 align:middle line:84%
And I have found it to be
kind of nice, because it also

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:58.030 align:middle line:84%
has an autograder, and
it lets you submit stuff

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:59.570 align:middle line:90%
for recitation in class.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:01.040 align:middle line:90%
And I found it's pretty great.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:03.970 align:middle line:84%
I like the interface,
but I just hate the fact

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:06.680 align:middle line:84%
that I have to bookmark every
single different website or else

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:07.355 align:middle line:90%
I forget them.

00:17:07.355 --> 00:17:08.230 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Uh-huh.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:08.630 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:09.963 align:middle line:90%
Some of those logistical things.

00:17:09.963 --> 00:17:12.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, to add
to that, different course--

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:14.530 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I've seen it
mostly in course 6.

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:19.609 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, different Course 6 classes
use it to different degrees.

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:24.099 align:middle line:84%
And there is a little
call for it there.

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:27.260 align:middle line:84%
There are some classes where
the entire class is on there.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:32.150 align:middle line:84%
And then you go to your class
in the basement with 40 people,

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.320 align:middle line:84%
and they're all trying to
log in at the same time

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:36.050 align:middle line:84%
so that they're
marked as present,

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:39.840 align:middle line:84%
and they're starting their lab,
and everything just crashes.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:42.620 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: That is
so comforting to me

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:45.000 align:middle line:84%
because I run this system
called Teacher Moments.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.410 align:middle line:90%
And we have similar problems.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:49.280 align:middle line:84%
But I'm not an MIT
Computer Science professor.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:54.750 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that their shit
doesn't work either is very--

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:56.943 align:middle line:90%
that's very comforting to me.

00:17:56.943 --> 00:17:58.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
that there's one thing

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:03.320 align:middle line:84%
that we kind of discussed, which
is an application of the MITx,

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.900 align:middle line:84%
is typically, most
freshmen at MIT

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:09.440 align:middle line:90%
take the 8.01, 8.02 TEAL series.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.560 align:middle line:84%
And I feel like that gives
this false advertisement of how

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:15.920 align:middle line:84%
education technology is used
here at MIT, where they give you

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.440 align:middle line:90%
this flipped classroom idea.

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:20.130 align:middle line:90%
And some kids love it.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:22.550 align:middle line:84%
And then you switch
into another class,

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:25.920 align:middle line:84%
and you never see that
type of learning again.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.480 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, well, I
liked that, so where is it?

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:32.400 align:middle line:84%
And for me, who
struggled through it

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:36.690 align:middle line:84%
because I was a student that
was kind of behind going in,

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:39.420 align:middle line:84%
then I switched to
my Course 16 classes,

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:42.098 align:middle line:84%
where it's a professor
lecturing in front of you,

00:18:42.098 --> 00:18:44.140 align:middle line:84%
and all of a sudden I now
understand the concept.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:46.290 align:middle line:84%
It's like, why did
I lose a whole year?

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:50.646 align:middle line:84%
So it's kind of like that
back and forth between,

00:18:50.646 --> 00:18:53.320 align:middle line:84%
are you going to continue to
implement this in other ways,

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:56.838 align:middle line:84%
or are we just stagnant in
this one idea behind it?

00:18:56.838 --> 00:18:57.630 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:18:57.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:58.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.720 align:middle line:84%
That's really-- I mean, TEAL is
an experience that a lot of you

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:01.220 align:middle line:90%
have had.

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:03.030 align:middle line:84%
If you're not from
here, TEAL is a way

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:05.730 align:middle line:84%
that they do introductory
physics teaching.

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:09.220 align:middle line:84%
It came about because
of high failure rates,

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:11.670 align:middle line:84%
particularly from people who
came from backgrounds where

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.830 align:middle line:84%
they were less well
prepared in physics.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:17.527 align:middle line:84%
And so 8.01, 8.02, these
introductory physics classes,

00:19:17.527 --> 00:19:19.860 align:middle line:84%
would be taught as lectures
with problem sets and things

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:20.700 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:22.752 align:middle line:84%
It also actually--
there's a long--

00:19:22.752 --> 00:19:24.960 align:middle line:84%
we won't get into it-- a
really interesting tradition

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.260 align:middle line:84%
of research in physics
education, which,

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:30.040 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, three
decades ago maybe,

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:32.830 align:middle line:84%
discovered that
when students, even

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:35.900 align:middle line:84%
at really good universities,
take introductory physics,

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:37.720 align:middle line:84%
historically they
were actually less

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:40.150 align:middle line:84%
Newtonian in their thinking
at the end of the class

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:42.190 align:middle line:90%
than at the beginning.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:43.330 align:middle line:90%
They got good grades.

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:46.490 align:middle line:84%
They could solve all the
kinds of physics problems.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:48.830 align:middle line:84%
But then they gave
them this test,

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:51.250 align:middle line:84%
this assessment called the
Force Concept Inventory, which

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.700 align:middle line:84%
was a bunch of conceptual
questions about how force works,

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:56.200 align:middle line:84%
and students would actually do
worse at the end of the class

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.700 align:middle line:84%
than they would do
at the beginning.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.258 align:middle line:84%
So there was this
sense that you could

00:20:00.258 --> 00:20:02.050 align:middle line:84%
spend a whole bunch of
time teaching people

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:04.133 align:middle line:84%
the procedures of physics,
and they would actually

00:20:04.133 --> 00:20:06.730 align:middle line:84%
come to slightly
more misunderstand

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:10.030 align:middle line:84%
the concepts as a result
of that, which is bad.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:15.303 align:middle line:84%
And so active learning,
learning environments

00:20:15.303 --> 00:20:17.470 align:middle line:84%
where students are asked
to not just listen and take

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.390 align:middle line:84%
notes and then do problem
sets, but to engage

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:21.490 align:middle line:84%
during the conversations
about the material,

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:22.850 align:middle line:90%
were shown to improve that.

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:25.120 align:middle line:84%
And then TEAL stuck around
because, while people

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:27.010 align:middle line:84%
have individual
experiences with it,

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:30.910 align:middle line:84%
there are fewer students who
failed 8.01 and 8.02 after TEAL

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:33.760 align:middle line:84%
was implemented and given a
few years to ramp up and work

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:35.710 align:middle line:90%
than there were beforehand.

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:39.790 align:middle line:84%
However, just
because, on average,

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:43.720 align:middle line:84%
the TEAL system is more likely
to have more students persist,

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.480 align:middle line:84%
doesn't mean that for
any individual student--

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:48.640 align:middle line:84%
individual students
can still not like it,

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:50.003 align:middle line:90%
and it can not serve them well.

00:20:50.003 --> 00:20:52.420 align:middle line:84%
And it can not serve them well
because of their background

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:53.740 align:middle line:90%
preparation or because--

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:55.870 align:middle line:84%
I mean, lots of people
who come to MIT end

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.287 align:middle line:84%
up here because they're pretty
good at teaching themselves

00:20:58.287 --> 00:20:59.410 align:middle line:90%
stuff through lectures.

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:02.933 align:middle line:84%
And so alternate methods,
when the physics department

00:21:02.933 --> 00:21:04.850 align:middle line:84%
is trying to think about
what to do with that,

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:05.950 align:middle line:84%
these are all the
kinds of trade-offs

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:07.220 align:middle line:90%
that they're considering.

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.280 align:middle line:84%
What were you going
to say, Howard?

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:10.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Slides,
especially when

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:13.850 align:middle line:84%
you're taking classes
that are Course 6 or math.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:15.880 align:middle line:84%
When professors
use slides, I feel

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:17.950 align:middle line:84%
like it affects my
learning because I like

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:21.680 align:middle line:90%
when they use chalk and board.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:23.740 align:middle line:84%
And it's just walking
through it because, one, it

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:24.680 align:middle line:90%
slows them down.

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:24.940 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:25.225 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:25.225 --> 00:21:27.517 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And two, you also
kind of catch up with them.

00:21:27.517 --> 00:21:29.850 align:middle line:84%
But if they're using
slides, they go very fast.

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:31.502 align:middle line:90%
And second, you just have to--

00:21:31.502 --> 00:21:32.960 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, it
makes the learning

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:34.088 align:middle line:90%
process a different thing.

00:21:34.088 --> 00:21:34.880 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:35.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:37.110 align:middle line:84%
No, I think it's
really interesting.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.170 align:middle line:84%
They're probably
like-- they're less

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:40.940 align:middle line:84%
likely to have
errors on the slides

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:43.223 align:middle line:84%
than they are when they're
working things out by hand.

00:21:43.223 --> 00:21:45.140 align:middle line:84%
However, it's not totally
clear whether or not

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:46.863 align:middle line:90%
errors are a bad thing in that.

00:21:46.863 --> 00:21:48.780 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes people note
the errors in real time,

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:49.940 align:middle line:84%
or they note them in
real time, and they

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:51.023 align:middle line:90%
fix them and discuss them.

00:21:51.023 --> 00:21:53.870 align:middle line:84%
But definitely, if you
can only talk and move

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:56.480 align:middle line:84%
at the pace at which
someone can write,

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.590 align:middle line:84%
that can have different
kind of advantages.

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:00.590 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the slides are
kind of cool in that they

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:02.442 align:middle line:90%
get automatically sent to you.

00:22:02.442 --> 00:22:03.900 align:middle line:84%
They can be sent
to you in advance.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.580 align:middle line:84%
You can take notes
directly on them.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:07.650 align:middle line:84%
Is taking notes directly
on them actually better,

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.610 align:middle line:84%
or is it better to have to hand
copy, pay attention to what's

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:11.710 align:middle line:90%
going on there?

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:13.460 align:middle line:84%
These are all great
questions, and they're

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:15.420 align:middle line:84%
all questions that actually
have research behind them.

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.220 align:middle line:84%
There are folks who come up with
experiments where they test all

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.470 align:middle line:90%
of these different conditions.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:20.720 align:middle line:84%
What do we do when
we tell students

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:22.080 align:middle line:90%
to try to copy everything down?

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:23.580 align:middle line:84%
What do we do when we tell
students to summarize?

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:24.840 align:middle line:84%
What if they give
them stuff in advance?

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:25.950 align:middle line:84%
What if we let them
have computers?

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:27.760 align:middle line:84%
What if we take away
their computers?

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:29.530 align:middle line:84%
Actually, some of
the best places

00:22:29.530 --> 00:22:32.950 align:middle line:84%
to do this research end
up being military schools,

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:35.117 align:middle line:84%
like West Point and
the Naval Academy

00:22:35.117 --> 00:22:37.450 align:middle line:84%
and stuff like that, because
they have a lot of required

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:39.280 align:middle line:84%
classes, and because
you can be pretty

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:42.100 align:middle line:84%
sure that the students will
do what you tell them to do.

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:43.600 align:middle line:84%
You assign them to
either condition,

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:47.170 align:middle line:90%
and they do what they want to.

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:48.430 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:51.587 align:middle line:84%
So an interesting thing
about our own experiences

00:22:51.587 --> 00:22:53.170 align:middle line:84%
is that our own
experiences are really

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:55.360 align:middle line:84%
important sources of
data for understanding

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:56.510 align:middle line:90%
education technology.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:59.650 align:middle line:84%
It's important for us
to look through the lens

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:01.670 align:middle line:90%
of our own learning experiences.

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:03.470 align:middle line:84%
And they can be
very informative.

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:06.080 align:middle line:84%
The other thing to remember is
that if you're studying at MIT,

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:07.840 align:middle line:90%
you're probably weird.

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:09.640 align:middle line:84%
There are probably
characteristics of you

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:10.850 align:middle line:90%
which are not typical.

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:15.190 align:middle line:84%
Like if you generally find
classroom learning pleasant,

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:16.870 align:middle line:90%
you are unusual.

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:19.360 align:middle line:84%
If you think to
yourself, man, I really

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:22.450 align:middle line:84%
like just kind of learning
stuff and figuring out on my own

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:25.060 align:middle line:84%
in academic material,
that is unusual.

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.830 align:middle line:84%
Most people are not
good at those things

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:28.250 align:middle line:90%
and don't find them enjoyable.

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.530 align:middle line:84%
So it can be helpful to
bring your own experience.

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:32.447 align:middle line:84%
But it's also important
to recognize the way--

00:23:32.447 --> 00:23:35.207 align:middle line:84%
and I went to private
schools my whole life.

00:23:35.207 --> 00:23:36.790 align:middle line:84%
I've had an
extraordinarily privileged

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.100 align:middle line:90%
educational upbringing.

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:41.530 align:middle line:84%
In lots of ways, I
have to recognize

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:43.870 align:middle line:84%
how that upbringing
does not prepare me well

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:46.180 align:middle line:84%
to understand the learning
experiences of most students

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:48.130 align:middle line:84%
who go to K-12 schools and
public schools in the United

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:48.980 align:middle line:90%
States and things like that.

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:51.200 align:middle line:84%
So be conscientious of that
as we do some thinking.

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:51.950 align:middle line:90%
But this stuff is good.

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:53.408 align:middle line:84%
All the things
you're talking about

00:23:53.408 --> 00:23:55.930 align:middle line:84%
are things we'll keep
trying to make sense of.

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:59.530 align:middle line:90%
Failure to Disrupt.

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:01.810 align:middle line:84%
This is a book about
large-scale learning,

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:04.930 align:middle line:84%
learning environments with many,
many learners and few experts

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:06.520 align:middle line:90%
to guide them.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:10.570 align:middle line:84%
And I would say,
the heart of what

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:17.470 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to do in this book
is to demystify education

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:21.370 align:middle line:84%
technology for
people and to say,

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:23.500 align:middle line:84%
with every generation
of new technologies,

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:26.340 align:middle line:84%
there will be folks who come
along that try to go to schools

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.420 align:middle line:84%
and say, this thing
is totally brand new.

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:30.690 align:middle line:90%
This is disruptive.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:31.960 align:middle line:90%
This is innovative.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:33.940 align:middle line:90%
This is a game changer.

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:36.090 align:middle line:84%
This is going to
transform education.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:39.540 align:middle line:84%
And my job is to be
like, eh, probably not.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:42.180 align:middle line:84%
It might be helpful, but
it's very, very unlikely

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:45.390 align:middle line:84%
that it will be any of
those kinds of things.

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:48.840 align:middle line:84%
One of the ways to
demystify technologies

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:51.750 align:middle line:84%
is to show where they
come from, is to say,

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:53.290 align:middle line:90%
these things have patterns.

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.530 align:middle line:90%
The patterns are recognizable.

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:58.450 align:middle line:84%
They are patterns, not just in
how these things are designed,

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:01.720 align:middle line:84%
but how they get implemented and
what kind of effects they have.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.460 align:middle line:84%
When new technologies
come along,

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:06.300 align:middle line:84%
we don't have to
guess out of the blue

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:07.660 align:middle line:90%
how well they might work.

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:10.092 align:middle line:84%
We know roughly how well
education technology

00:25:10.092 --> 00:25:11.800 align:middle line:84%
works in different
kinds of circumstance.

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:13.590 align:middle line:84%
Now, new things-- there
will be new things

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.548 align:middle line:84%
that are invented that
are, in fact, different.

00:25:15.548 --> 00:25:18.460 align:middle line:84%
But most new things are
just a little bit new.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:21.410 align:middle line:84%
They make just small
modifications, small advances

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:25.010 align:middle line:90%
off of past things.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:28.280 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
half of the book

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:32.180 align:middle line:84%
confronts the challenge, why
is it so hard to make progress

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:33.480 align:middle line:90%
with education technology?

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:35.370 align:middle line:90%
Why are things not disruptive?

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:36.720 align:middle line:90%
Why are they not transformative?

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:38.690 align:middle line:90%
Why are they not game changers?

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:41.040 align:middle line:84%
And I think there are
answers to those questions.

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:43.850 align:middle line:84%
I think there are challenges
that we have as a field yet

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.920 align:middle line:90%
to figure out how to tackle.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:48.780 align:middle line:84%
And if we study those challenges
and make sense of them,

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:51.540 align:middle line:84%
then we could probably do a
better job moving past them.

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.860 align:middle line:84%
So the first half of the book
looks at these three genres

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:56.010 align:middle line:90%
of learning at scale.

00:25:56.010 --> 00:25:58.640 align:middle line:84%
It says that most education
technologies, especially

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:00.740 align:middle line:84%
the ones where you try
to teach a lot of people

00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:04.170 align:middle line:84%
with very few experts, fall
into one of three buckets.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:05.600 align:middle line:84%
And you can figure
out what bucket

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:08.240 align:middle line:84%
each thing is in by
asking the question, who

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:10.790 align:middle line:84%
guides the sequence of
learning activities?

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:12.560 align:middle line:84%
When learners do
stuff, they happen

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:14.840 align:middle line:84%
in an order, one after
the other after the other,

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:17.330 align:middle line:84%
and there are really
three sorts of ways

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:19.070 align:middle line:90%
that those orders unfold.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:21.700 align:middle line:84%
The first is instructor-guided
learning at scale.

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:24.480 align:middle line:84%
So there can be some expert
or team of experts who says,

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.500 align:middle line:84%
you should learn
things in this order.

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:29.500 align:middle line:84%
Here's part 1, part 2,
part 3, part 4, part 5.

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:31.390 align:middle line:84%
And maybe learners
don't go in that order,

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:33.100 align:middle line:90%
but overwhelmingly they will.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:35.050 align:middle line:84%
And there's some logical
sequence to that.

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:36.960 align:middle line:84%
So I call those things
instructor-guided

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:38.490 align:middle line:90%
learning at scale.

00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:40.240 align:middle line:84%
A bunch of what you
read about for today--

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:42.450 align:middle line:84%
massive open online
courses, any kind

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:45.930 align:middle line:84%
of self-paced online learning
falls in that bucket.

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:48.510 align:middle line:84%
The second big genre
is algorithm-guided

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:49.600 align:middle line:90%
learning at scale.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:53.920 align:middle line:84%
So that's when a computer
poses you a problem,

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:56.040 align:middle line:84%
you answer that problem,
and the computer

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:57.490 align:middle line:90%
evaluates your performance.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:59.650 align:middle line:84%
And then based on
that performance,

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:03.150 align:middle line:84%
it gives you either more
difficult problems or simpler

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:04.950 align:middle line:84%
problems, or the
same kind of problem,

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:07.240 align:middle line:84%
or shunts you in some kind
of different direction.

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:10.300 align:middle line:84%
But basically, the computer
evaluates your performance,

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:12.510 align:middle line:84%
decides on the basis of
that performance what

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:15.190 align:middle line:84%
the next item in a sequence
of activities should be,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:19.210 align:middle line:90%
and then you go do that forever.

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:21.080 align:middle line:90%
You are probably--

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:22.130 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.550 align:middle line:84%
When I taught this 10
years ago, maybe students

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:26.240 align:middle line:84%
would have dabbled
with these things,

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:28.180 align:middle line:84%
but it's far, far
more common now.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:34.240 align:middle line:84%
If you in school ever used
DreamBox or IXL or Khan Academy,

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:36.310 align:middle line:84%
almost all of those
things are some version

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:39.010 align:middle line:84%
of algorithm-guided
learning at scale.

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:40.790 align:middle line:84%
A bunch of the testing
that you've done,

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:42.560 align:middle line:84%
if you've taken the
computer-based GREs,

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:45.340 align:middle line:84%
other things like that, they
use similar technologies.

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:48.430 align:middle line:84%
The third bucket of things
we call peer-guided learning

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:49.193 align:middle line:90%
at scale.

00:27:49.193 --> 00:27:50.860 align:middle line:84%
So if any of you have
ever played around

00:27:50.860 --> 00:27:52.820 align:middle line:84%
with the Scratch
programming community,

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:54.680 align:middle line:84%
there's no instruction
in Scratch.

00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:57.760 align:middle line:84%
There's no Scratch
University or Scratch lesson

00:27:57.760 --> 00:27:58.910 align:middle line:90%
1, lesson 2, lesson 3.

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:00.860 align:middle line:84%
And that's very
intentional and by design.

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:04.780 align:middle line:84%
Instead, you're immersed
in this community of peers.

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:07.250 align:middle line:84%
And if you go to the home
page of the Scratch website,

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:10.230 align:middle line:84%
there are featured tutorials
and featured projects and things

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:10.730 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:12.160 align:middle line:84%
And the way you
figure out Scratch

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.030 align:middle line:84%
is that you think about some
things that you want to do,

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:15.100 align:middle line:84%
and then you find some
other people who've

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:17.010 align:middle line:84%
done that kind of thing,
and you copy them,

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:20.070 align:middle line:84%
you converse with them, you take
their project and you modify it,

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:23.360 align:middle line:84%
but you basically
design your own pathway.

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:27.600 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of other learning
environments, Stack Overflow,

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:29.660 align:middle line:84%
other kinds of
communities of practice.

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:31.910 align:middle line:84%
The one that I talk about
at the beginning of the book

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:33.050 align:middle line:90%
is Rainbow Loom.

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:35.550 align:middle line:84%
Any of you ever played with
Rainbow Loom when you were kids?

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:36.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:38.490 align:middle line:84%
So when you bought a-- did
I tell you this story yet?

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:39.550 align:middle line:84%
When you bought a
Rainbow Loom, there

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:40.900 align:middle line:90%
were 10 cards in the packet.

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:42.150 align:middle line:90%
So you pulled open the packet.

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.690 align:middle line:84%
There's a bunch of these
little rubber bands, a little--

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:45.960 align:middle line:90%
what do they call that thing?

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.270 align:middle line:84%
Frame, to stitch
the rubber bands on.

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:51.498 align:middle line:84%
And then there are 10 cards
that show 10 different kinds

00:28:51.498 --> 00:28:52.790 align:middle line:90%
of bracelets that you can make.

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:54.950 align:middle line:84%
If you get really
into Rainbow Loom,

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:56.697 align:middle line:84%
10 different kinds
of bracelets is not

00:28:56.697 --> 00:28:57.780 align:middle line:90%
enough kinds of bracelets.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:00.260 align:middle line:84%
You have to learn more
different ways of doing it.

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:03.440 align:middle line:84%
And the main way you'll learn
that is through YouTube.

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:04.585 align:middle line:90%
You'll go to this--

00:29:04.585 --> 00:29:05.960 align:middle line:84%
and here's the
thing that boggled

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:07.085 align:middle line:90%
my mind about Rainbow Loom.

00:29:07.085 --> 00:29:09.080 align:middle line:84%
So there's some dude
who is in Michigan.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:10.830 align:middle line:84%
He was an automotive
engineer or something

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.413 align:middle line:84%
like that who was
like, in my country,

00:29:12.413 --> 00:29:13.880 align:middle line:84%
this is the way
we make bracelets.

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:16.980 align:middle line:84%
And he comes up with
some CAD designs,

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:18.280 align:middle line:90%
and he sends them to China.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:20.290 align:middle line:84%
And China manufactures
zillions of these things.

00:29:20.290 --> 00:29:21.998 align:middle line:84%
And they put them on
shipping containers,

00:29:21.998 --> 00:29:24.340 align:middle line:84%
and they send them to
his distribution center.

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:26.985 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, these
packets, these little boxes

00:29:26.985 --> 00:29:29.110 align:middle line:84%
of Rainbow Loom things get
sent all over the world.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.290 align:middle line:84%
It's a total supply
chain miracle.

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.748 align:middle line:84%
The fact that some dude in
Michigan just has an idea

00:29:33.748 --> 00:29:35.790 align:middle line:84%
and is like, let's have
people all over the world

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:37.630 align:middle line:84%
buy these things, and
they do, is amazing.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:41.220 align:middle line:84%
But simultaneously to
that physical supply chain

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:46.050 align:middle line:84%
infrastructure, this giant
global community of Rainbow Loom

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:48.540 align:middle line:84%
learners emerges,
where there are

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:52.890 align:middle line:84%
people who are running
little classes at art shops,

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:54.390 align:middle line:84%
and they're posting
videos online,

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:56.140 align:middle line:84%
and they're sharing
ideas with each other,

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:58.500 align:middle line:84%
and they're creating little
Facebook Marketplaces.

00:29:58.500 --> 00:30:01.080 align:middle line:84%
And the only way
Rainbow Loom could grow

00:30:01.080 --> 00:30:04.690 align:middle line:84%
is if that learning
network grows alongside it.

00:30:04.690 --> 00:30:06.390 align:middle line:90%
That is miraculous.

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:09.540 align:middle line:84%
That is an amazing feature
of online learning,

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:14.050 align:middle line:84%
that people could invent a tool
to do this kind of practice

00:30:14.050 --> 00:30:17.800 align:middle line:84%
and a community could develop to
teach each other and themselves

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:18.910 align:middle line:90%
this practice.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:19.923 align:middle line:90%
I think there were--

00:30:19.923 --> 00:30:21.340 align:middle line:84%
oh, the other thing
I really liked

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:22.923 align:middle line:84%
about that, which I
think I mentioned,

00:30:22.923 --> 00:30:25.368 align:middle line:84%
is that there were
these two girls--

00:30:25.368 --> 00:30:27.160 align:middle line:84%
I think their names
were Ashley and Steph--

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:29.420 align:middle line:84%
who made Rainbow
Loom YouTube videos.

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:31.690 align:middle line:90%
And they got millions of views.

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:35.620 align:middle line:84%
And you could compare their
videos to the actual Rainbow

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:37.190 align:middle line:90%
Loom manufacturer videos.

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:39.580 align:middle line:84%
And these girls were getting
like 10x the number of views

00:30:39.580 --> 00:30:41.260 align:middle line:90%
that the actual Rainbow--

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:43.480 align:middle line:84%
basically, kids
would rather learn

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:44.757 align:middle line:90%
from these two random girls.

00:30:44.757 --> 00:30:46.340 align:middle line:84%
They ended up being
from Philadelphia.

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.280 align:middle line:84%
They're very good at protecting
their anonymity, at least

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:48.780 align:middle line:90%
for a while.

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:51.010 align:middle line:84%
One of them eventually
became a makeup influencer,

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:52.850 align:middle line:90%
and then they revealed herself.

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:58.565 align:middle line:84%
But I find these global networks
incredibly fascinating and very,

00:30:58.565 --> 00:31:00.190 align:middle line:84%
very difficult to
integrate in schools,

00:31:00.190 --> 00:31:03.460 align:middle line:84%
for reasons that we'll
explore as we go along.

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:07.240 align:middle line:84%
So if you buy that there are
these three big categories,

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:09.730 align:middle line:84%
a thing that we'll do
over the next few weeks

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:12.640 align:middle line:84%
is we'll look at how
there are similarities

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.800 align:middle line:90%
within these categories.

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:17.410 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, in
instructor-guided learning

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:21.335 align:middle line:84%
at scale, there typically
are two key technologies.

00:31:21.335 --> 00:31:22.960 align:middle line:84%
There are two
technologies that show up

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:24.610 align:middle line:90%
over and over and over again.

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:26.480 align:middle line:84%
One is a learning
management system.

00:31:26.480 --> 00:31:27.860 align:middle line:84%
So a learning
management system--

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:31.012 align:middle line:84%
Canvas, Blackboard,
edX, Moodle, Stellar,

00:31:31.012 --> 00:31:32.470 align:middle line:84%
or a zillion other
things-- they're

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:36.760 align:middle line:84%
platforms that organize
learning materials.

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:39.610 align:middle line:84%
An unusual feature of
learning management systems

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:44.170 align:middle line:84%
is that they have a feature that
people who study products called

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:45.440 align:middle line:90%
feature convergence.

00:31:45.440 --> 00:31:49.600 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, it's very
difficult to patent or have

00:31:49.600 --> 00:31:52.240 align:middle line:84%
intellectual property around
components of a learning

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:53.000 align:middle line:90%
management system.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:54.830 align:middle line:84%
So anytime anyone
invents something good,

00:31:54.830 --> 00:31:56.530 align:middle line:90%
everybody else copies it.

00:31:56.530 --> 00:31:59.510 align:middle line:84%
So they're all basically
the exact same system.

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:01.010 align:middle line:84%
You buy them from
different vendors,

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:04.030 align:middle line:84%
but they're basically one
learning management system.

00:32:04.030 --> 00:32:07.180 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
kind of crucial feature

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:09.190 align:middle line:90%
of online learning management--

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:11.990 align:middle line:84%
or instructor-guided learning
at scale are autograders.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:13.910 align:middle line:84%
So you have to be able
to give-- if you have

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:16.160 align:middle line:84%
lots and lots of learners, if
you want to be able to give them

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:17.870 align:middle line:84%
feedback, if you
want to be to certify

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:19.872 align:middle line:84%
their learning in
any way, you either

00:32:19.872 --> 00:32:21.830 align:middle line:84%
have to spend zillions
of dollars hiring people

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:25.610 align:middle line:84%
to grade their work, or you find
ways of having computers grade

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:26.130 align:middle line:90%
their work.

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:28.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, computers can-- we'll
talk about this at length.

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:31.220 align:middle line:84%
Computers can grade some
things, but not others.

00:32:31.220 --> 00:32:33.050 align:middle line:84%
Computers are really
good at answering--

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:36.050 align:middle line:84%
at evaluating questions
where the answer is

00:32:36.050 --> 00:32:37.670 align:middle line:90%
well known and certain.

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:39.710 align:middle line:84%
They are much less
good at evaluating

00:32:39.710 --> 00:32:43.670 align:middle line:84%
anything which has a kind of
subjective component to it.

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:45.620 align:middle line:84%
One way to think about
this is, autograders

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:48.350 align:middle line:84%
are great in early
language acquisition

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:52.370 align:middle line:84%
and very rapidly become useless
in later language acquisition.

00:32:52.370 --> 00:32:56.187 align:middle line:84%
So they can tell whether you've
spelled "hola" correctly.

00:32:56.187 --> 00:32:58.520 align:middle line:84%
We even have some pretty cool
ones that can tell whether

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.327 align:middle line:84%
you've pronounced
"hola" correctly.

00:33:00.327 --> 00:33:02.660 align:middle line:84%
We can give you a multiple
choice question to see if you

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:05.750 align:middle line:84%
can recognize the audio
recording of "hola."

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:08.240 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, however,
what we want to know are,

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:10.680 align:middle line:84%
what are the key
themes in Don Quixote?

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.990 align:middle line:84%
And that we don't have
very good autograders to be

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:16.170 align:middle line:90%
able to evaluate and so forth.

00:33:16.170 --> 00:33:19.390 align:middle line:84%
I would say, in addition to this
technological infrastructure,

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:21.100 align:middle line:84%
these genres of
learning at scale,

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:25.950 align:middle line:84%
they tend to align more closely
either with direct instruction

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:30.722 align:middle line:84%
kind of folks or apprenticeship
constructivism kinds of folks.

00:33:30.722 --> 00:33:32.680 align:middle line:84%
The instructor-guided
learning at scale people,

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:35.920 align:middle line:84%
they tend to be Thorndike,
direct instruction,

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:37.840 align:middle line:84%
cognitive load theory
kind of people.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:42.180 align:middle line:84%
They tend to be
pail-filling kinds of folks.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.760 align:middle line:84%
Algorithm-guided
learning at scale

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.220 align:middle line:84%
has similarities
but differences.

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:52.170 align:middle line:84%
So the crucial technology
behind algorithm-guided learning

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:55.590 align:middle line:84%
at scale is a set of algorithms
called item response theory.

00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:59.445 align:middle line:84%
This was developed in the 1980s
by Educational Testing Services,

00:33:59.445 --> 00:34:00.945 align:middle line:84%
the people who
make-- what do they--

00:34:00.945 --> 00:34:02.940 align:middle line:90%
do they do the SAT or the GRE?

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:05.198 align:middle line:90%
What test does ETS make?

00:34:05.198 --> 00:34:05.740 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:34:05.740 --> 00:34:07.210 align:middle line:90%
They make tests.

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:14.500 align:middle line:84%
And the basic toolkit
of item response theory

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:17.330 align:middle line:90%
has proven really quite robust.

00:34:17.330 --> 00:34:19.570 align:middle line:84%
All item response
theory does basically

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:22.405 align:middle line:84%
is it lets you figure out
how difficult an item is.

00:34:22.405 --> 00:34:24.530 align:middle line:84%
It's a way of quantifying
how difficult an item is.

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:26.469 align:middle line:84%
If you can quantify how
difficult an item is, if someone

00:34:26.469 --> 00:34:28.480 align:middle line:84%
gets the item right, you can
give them a more difficult item

00:34:28.480 --> 00:34:29.449 align:middle line:90%
or less difficult item.

00:34:29.449 --> 00:34:30.949 align:middle line:84%
It was actually
originally developed

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:34.510 align:middle line:84%
so that you could give people
tests of equivalent difficulty

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:36.620 align:middle line:84%
without giving
them the same test.

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:39.550 align:middle line:84%
This proved important, like
when we had national testing

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.469 align:middle line:84%
and we built
communication networks,

00:34:41.469 --> 00:34:45.080 align:middle line:84%
because, in theory, somebody
could take a test in Portland,

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:47.679 align:middle line:84%
Maine, and later that same
day, someone would take it

00:34:47.679 --> 00:34:49.058 align:middle line:90%
in Honolulu, Hawaii.

00:34:49.058 --> 00:34:51.100 align:middle line:84%
And there's just enough
time in there for someone

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:53.770 align:middle line:84%
to call and send all the
questions and answers

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:55.570 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:34:55.570 --> 00:35:00.080 align:middle line:84%
And so it, in part, came
about from test security.

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:04.415 align:middle line:84%
You want to be able to make
claims about people's ability.

00:35:04.415 --> 00:35:06.790 align:middle line:84%
And one way to do that is to
give everyone the same test.

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:08.790 align:middle line:84%
Another way to do that
is to give everyone tests

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.220 align:middle line:84%
with different items but
of the same difficulty.

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:13.470 align:middle line:84%
And so we had to come up
with some statistical toolkit

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:15.990 align:middle line:90%
for doing that.

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:19.620 align:middle line:84%
What we came up with
in the 1980s and 1990s,

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:22.950 align:middle line:84%
we've modified it a bit, but
we still pretty much use today.

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:25.860 align:middle line:84%
We know a bunch about its
strengths and weaknesses

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:27.090 align:middle line:90%
and how it works.

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:29.565 align:middle line:84%
Most of our autograders,
just now maybe they're

00:35:29.565 --> 00:35:30.940 align:middle line:84%
starting to improve
a little bit.

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:33.232 align:middle line:84%
Most of them haven't improved
that much in the last two

00:35:33.232 --> 00:35:34.950 align:middle line:90%
or three decades.

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:37.617 align:middle line:84%
In some specific areas, some
parts of language acquisition,

00:35:37.617 --> 00:35:40.200 align:middle line:84%
some parts of computer science,
maybe they've improved better.

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:43.500 align:middle line:84%
But these technologies, for the
most part, are pretty stable.

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:45.660 align:middle line:90%
When someone has a new entrant--

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:48.160 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you'll read more
of this story in chapter 2.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:50.220 align:middle line:84%
But there was a
company called Newton,

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:54.570 align:middle line:84%
which said that they could take
any body of educational content

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:57.240 align:middle line:84%
and turn it into an
intelligent tutor.

00:35:57.240 --> 00:35:59.890 align:middle line:84%
And they thought that their
tutor was absolutely amazing.

00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:02.610 align:middle line:84%
The founder described
it as something

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:04.410 align:middle line:84%
like a magical robot
tutor in the sky,

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:06.850 align:middle line:84%
knew exactly what you were
thinking and so forth.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:09.610 align:middle line:84%
So he was making all these
totally outlandish claims.

00:36:09.610 --> 00:36:12.920 align:middle line:84%
And then his
engineers had a blog

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:14.170 align:middle line:90%
about what they were building.

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:16.128 align:middle line:84%
And one of their blog
posts was something like,

00:36:16.128 --> 00:36:18.120 align:middle line:84%
using two-parameter
item response theory

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:19.120 align:middle line:90%
to evaluate items.

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:20.477 align:middle line:90%
They were basically like--

00:36:20.477 --> 00:36:22.060 align:middle line:84%
so the leader is out
there being like,

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:23.650 align:middle line:90%
this thing is totally amazing.

00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:25.530 align:middle line:84%
And the engineers
are like, here's

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:27.030 align:middle line:84%
the very well-established
technology

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:31.530 align:middle line:84%
that we're using to do the thing
that we are selling you to do.

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:33.720 align:middle line:84%
The peer-guided
learning at scale

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:36.095 align:middle line:84%
looks pretty different
from the other two.

00:36:36.095 --> 00:36:37.470 align:middle line:84%
Peer-guided learning
environments

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:39.460 align:middle line:84%
tend to be more
apprenticeship-oriented.

00:36:39.460 --> 00:36:42.120 align:middle line:84%
They tend to be more wrapped
into situated learning, things

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:43.050 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:36:43.050 --> 00:36:47.370 align:middle line:84%
They tend to have a different
technical infrastructure.

00:36:47.370 --> 00:36:50.112 align:middle line:84%
Instead of being based in
some kind of content holding

00:36:50.112 --> 00:36:51.820 align:middle line:84%
thing, like a learning
management system,

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:53.490 align:middle line:90%
they usually exist on the web.

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:56.490 align:middle line:84%
And usually, their killer app is
not an autograder, but a thing

00:36:56.490 --> 00:36:58.230 align:middle line:90%
that pulls content together.

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:01.470 align:middle line:84%
How do you figure out what the
right sequence of Rainbow Loom

00:37:01.470 --> 00:37:04.380 align:middle line:84%
learning materials is to look
at, and things like hashtags

00:37:04.380 --> 00:37:07.410 align:middle line:90%
are really important for that.

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:10.088 align:middle line:84%
In Scratch, how do you
pull together and come up

00:37:10.088 --> 00:37:12.630 align:middle line:84%
with a set of featured projects,
or a way of tagging projects

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:14.700 align:middle line:84%
so that people can find
their way through them?

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So these are the
three big buckets

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:20.280 align:middle line:84%
that we'll explore
one bucket each

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:23.880 align:middle line:90%
over the next bunch of weeks.

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:26.340 align:middle line:84%
The second half
of the book, which

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:30.070 align:middle line:84%
I guess if this project takes
us a good chunk of March,

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:32.070 align:middle line:84%
this project will take
us a good chunk of April,

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:35.340 align:middle line:84%
are four problems that educators
and education technology

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.440 align:middle line:84%
designers run into
over and over again,

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:39.800 align:middle line:84%
which I'll enumerate
quickly just to orient you.

00:37:39.800 --> 00:37:42.780 align:middle line:90%


00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:43.420 align:middle line:90%
One's missing.

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:46.260 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the first one is
the Curse of the Familiar.

00:37:46.260 --> 00:37:48.030 align:middle line:84%
The idea of the
Curse of the Familiar

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:50.790 align:middle line:84%
is that it's pretty easy
to make technologies

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:53.260 align:middle line:84%
that do things that
educators already are doing

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:54.660 align:middle line:90%
and get them adopted.

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:57.300 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have
ever used Quizlet?

00:37:57.300 --> 00:37:59.028 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:37:59.028 --> 00:38:00.570 align:middle line:90%
That's about as universal--

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:03.220 align:middle line:84%
Quizlet was founded
by MIT dropout Andy

00:38:03.220 --> 00:38:07.550 align:middle line:84%
Sutherland, who took a bunch
of education classes with us.

00:38:07.550 --> 00:38:13.240 align:middle line:84%
And he built it to, I think,
study French for himself.

00:38:13.240 --> 00:38:15.100 align:middle line:90%
Quizlet is flashcards.

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:17.980 align:middle line:84%
If you went and you gathered
a bunch of educational experts

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:22.750 align:middle line:84%
around the world and said,
what do our kids really

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:24.057 align:middle line:90%
need to advance their learning?

00:38:24.057 --> 00:38:26.390 align:middle line:84%
There is no one who would
raise their hands and be like,

00:38:26.390 --> 00:38:28.040 align:middle line:84%
there's a huge
dearth of flashcards.

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:30.290 align:middle line:84%
We just don't have enough
flashcards at school.

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:33.100 align:middle line:84%
We've run out of index
cards, something like that.

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:36.167 align:middle line:90%
And yet here's this something--

00:38:36.167 --> 00:38:38.500 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they say things like,
half of all high schoolers

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.480 align:middle line:90%
every month use Quizlet.

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:43.400 align:middle line:84%
How much does Quizlet improve
teaching and learning?

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:45.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you're replacing
regular flashcards

00:38:45.910 --> 00:38:48.370 align:middle line:84%
with digital flashcards,
how much would

00:38:48.370 --> 00:38:50.230 align:middle line:84%
you expect that to
improve learning?

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:50.733 align:middle line:90%
Maybe some.

00:38:50.733 --> 00:38:52.400 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's a little
efficiency there,

00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:53.150 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:38:53.150 --> 00:38:55.150 align:middle line:84%
You also might say something
like, actually, one

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:56.830 align:middle line:84%
of the best things
about flashcards

00:38:56.830 --> 00:38:58.350 align:middle line:84%
is the creation
of the flashcards.

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:00.350 align:middle line:84%
There's actually something
important of learning

00:39:00.350 --> 00:39:02.392 align:middle line:84%
about writing down the
prompts on one end,

00:39:02.392 --> 00:39:03.600 align:middle line:90%
the answers on the other end.

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:05.100 align:middle line:84%
So maybe the paper
ones are actually

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:07.890 align:middle line:84%
better than the Quizlet ones
in different kinds of ways.

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:09.650 align:middle line:84%
But the point is,
there's probably

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:12.950 align:middle line:84%
no one who would make a bet
that human development would

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:16.350 align:middle line:84%
be profoundly transformed
by digitizing flashcards.

00:39:16.350 --> 00:39:19.002 align:middle line:84%
But if you look across many of
the most successful education

00:39:19.002 --> 00:39:21.210 align:middle line:84%
technology companies, they
do something like Quizlet.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:23.700 align:middle line:84%
They take a thing which is
already happening in schools,

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:26.690 align:middle line:84%
and they digitize it, maybe make
it a little bit more efficient.

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:28.190 align:middle line:84%
There are a handful
of projects that

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:30.860 align:middle line:84%
try to do something totally
different, and many of them

00:39:30.860 --> 00:39:31.590 align:middle line:90%
fail.

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:34.430 align:middle line:84%
And the reason why they
fail is it's really hard

00:39:34.430 --> 00:39:37.650 align:middle line:84%
to convince people in schools
to adopt totally new routines.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:40.910 align:middle line:84%
So there are all kinds of
visionary educators out there

00:39:40.910 --> 00:39:42.440 align:middle line:84%
who think, man,
wouldn't it be cool

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:44.550 align:middle line:84%
if we could teach math in this
really different kind of way,

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:46.670 align:middle line:84%
if we can teach computer science
in this really different kind

00:39:46.670 --> 00:39:47.180 align:middle line:90%
of way?

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:49.430 align:middle line:84%
And then they build things
that do that and people go,

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:50.443 align:middle line:90%
that's weird.

00:39:50.443 --> 00:39:52.860 align:middle line:84%
I don't understand this really
new, different kind of way.

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:55.360 align:middle line:84%
So a bunch of the things which
might potentially lead us

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:57.860 align:middle line:84%
to the biggest gains or changes
in our teaching and learning

00:39:57.860 --> 00:39:59.485 align:middle line:84%
are really hard to
get people to adopt.

00:39:59.485 --> 00:40:01.780 align:middle line:84%
That's the Curse
of the Familiar.

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:05.170 align:middle line:84%
The EdTech Matthew Effect is
that we very often hope that new

00:40:05.170 --> 00:40:08.110 align:middle line:84%
technologies, especially
free technologies, will be--

00:40:08.110 --> 00:40:10.690 align:middle line:84%
the term people often
use is "democratizing,"

00:40:10.690 --> 00:40:13.960 align:middle line:84%
that they will-- essentially
what will happen--

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:15.430 align:middle line:84%
people, generally
speaking, agreed

00:40:15.430 --> 00:40:17.020 align:middle line:84%
that folks who
have more resources

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:18.670 align:middle line:84%
have had more
learning experiences,

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:20.462 align:middle line:84%
and therefore have
learned more than people

00:40:20.462 --> 00:40:22.030 align:middle line:90%
with less experience.

00:40:22.030 --> 00:40:23.710 align:middle line:84%
But we hope that
there are technologies

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:27.920 align:middle line:84%
that will disproportionately
benefit the less affluent,

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:30.260 align:middle line:84%
that they will help poor folks
catch up to other people.

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:31.730 align:middle line:84%
They'll create new
kinds of on ramps.

00:40:31.730 --> 00:40:33.730 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they help everybody,
but they particularly

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:34.420 align:middle line:90%
help this group.

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:37.570 align:middle line:84%
What we almost always find is
the figure on the right, which

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:39.700 align:middle line:84%
is that new technologies
disproportionately benefit

00:40:39.700 --> 00:40:41.530 align:middle line:84%
the affluent, is
that they're very

00:40:41.530 --> 00:40:43.600 align:middle line:84%
likely to accelerate
the learning of people

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:45.880 align:middle line:90%
who are already advantaged.

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:48.130 align:middle line:84%
Many people go into
education because they're

00:40:48.130 --> 00:40:51.470 align:middle line:84%
trying to build a more
equitable, just society,

00:40:51.470 --> 00:40:54.550 align:middle line:84%
and it can be quite frustrating
if you keep building stuff

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:56.920 align:middle line:84%
and it keeps helping the most
affluent people in society

00:40:56.920 --> 00:41:00.160 align:middle line:90%
secure their position better.

00:41:00.160 --> 00:41:02.840 align:middle line:84%
We can document
this really well.

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:04.377 align:middle line:84%
It's actually, in
a lot of cases,

00:41:04.377 --> 00:41:05.960 align:middle line:84%
hard to understand
why it's happening.

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:08.050 align:middle line:84%
And we have very little
science or research

00:41:08.050 --> 00:41:11.708 align:middle line:84%
that helps us explain what
alternatives would be.

00:41:11.708 --> 00:41:13.750 align:middle line:84%
We'll give you some examples
of some technologies

00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:15.167 align:middle line:84%
that I think don't
do that, that I

00:41:15.167 --> 00:41:18.002 align:middle line:84%
think do disproportionately
benefit people further

00:41:18.002 --> 00:41:18.710 align:middle line:90%
from opportunity.

00:41:18.710 --> 00:41:22.180 align:middle line:84%
But it's really hard to
figure out why they work.

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:25.300 align:middle line:84%
A third challenge that
education technology folks

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:27.880 align:middle line:84%
come across all the
time is that computers

00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:30.140 align:middle line:84%
are good at evaluating
certain kinds of things.

00:41:30.140 --> 00:41:32.557 align:middle line:84%
The kinds of things that they're
really good at evaluating

00:41:32.557 --> 00:41:36.260 align:middle line:84%
are those that are routine, that
have common features to them,

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:38.800 align:middle line:90%
that are highly structured.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:42.730 align:middle line:84%
Computers are much less good at
evaluating ill structured kinds

00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:45.770 align:middle line:84%
of things, things that don't
have much form to them,

00:41:45.770 --> 00:41:48.130 align:middle line:84%
or we don't know what
inputs need to go in them

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:50.290 align:middle line:84%
or what the final
output should look like.

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:52.450 align:middle line:84%
For instance, one of
the things that we

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:55.240 align:middle line:84%
don't have good autograders
for is people's ability

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:58.580 align:middle line:84%
to reason from evidence
in natural language,

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:00.120 align:middle line:90%
either written or auditory.

00:42:00.120 --> 00:42:02.990 align:middle line:84%
The main thing that we
teach you for 16 or 20

00:42:02.990 --> 00:42:05.810 align:middle line:84%
years in the education system
is how to reason from evidence.

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:08.300 align:middle line:84%
The most important things
we're teaching you how to do we

00:42:08.300 --> 00:42:09.870 align:middle line:90%
don't have good autograders for.

00:42:09.870 --> 00:42:13.280 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, the kinds of things
that computers can grade

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:15.980 align:middle line:84%
tend to overlap pretty closely
with the kinds of things

00:42:15.980 --> 00:42:17.690 align:middle line:90%
that computers are good at.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:19.483 align:middle line:84%
If computers are
good at things, it's

00:42:19.483 --> 00:42:20.900 align:middle line:84%
going to be very
difficult to make

00:42:20.900 --> 00:42:22.470 align:middle line:90%
a living doing those things.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:25.610 align:middle line:84%
So we have really good tests
for the kinds of things

00:42:25.610 --> 00:42:28.670 align:middle line:84%
that are probably not so
important to teach people,

00:42:28.670 --> 00:42:30.713 align:middle line:84%
and we have not very
good tests on the kinds

00:42:30.713 --> 00:42:32.880 align:middle line:84%
of things that are really
important to teach people.

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:36.270 align:middle line:84%
That is a bad feature for
an educational system,

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:39.080 align:middle line:84%
because autograding of
all kinds is way cheaper

00:42:39.080 --> 00:42:40.460 align:middle line:90%
than human grading.

00:42:40.460 --> 00:42:42.770 align:middle line:84%
So we essentially build
all of these systems

00:42:42.770 --> 00:42:46.010 align:middle line:84%
where, conceivably,
we're spending money

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:49.308 align:middle line:84%
to cheaply evaluate the least
important parts of the system.

00:42:49.308 --> 00:42:51.350 align:middle line:84%
And we're not evaluating
the most important parts

00:42:51.350 --> 00:42:53.870 align:middle line:84%
that people ought to be
developing in those systems.

00:42:53.870 --> 00:42:57.020 align:middle line:84%
So that is the trap
of routine assessment.

00:42:57.020 --> 00:42:59.240 align:middle line:84%
A really cool thing
about computers

00:42:59.240 --> 00:43:01.370 align:middle line:84%
is that they can collect
enormous amounts of data

00:43:01.370 --> 00:43:05.030 align:middle line:84%
about what folks do and that we
can run experiments on people

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:06.450 align:middle line:90%
and improve those systems.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.900 align:middle line:84%
So every time-- not every
time, but 1% of the time

00:43:08.900 --> 00:43:11.252 align:middle line:84%
you go to Google or Amazon
or Facebook or whatever,

00:43:11.252 --> 00:43:13.460 align:middle line:84%
they're doing some little
randomized controlled trial

00:43:13.460 --> 00:43:13.980 align:middle line:90%
on you.

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:16.035 align:middle line:84%
The Buy button is
green instead of red.

00:43:16.035 --> 00:43:18.410 align:middle line:84%
And we figure out whether you
buy more from green buttons

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:19.340 align:middle line:90%
than red buttons.

00:43:19.340 --> 00:43:22.678 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, Amazon,
Facebook, they do millions.

00:43:22.678 --> 00:43:23.220 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:25.550 align:middle line:84%
They do many, many of those
experiments every year,

00:43:25.550 --> 00:43:28.340 align:middle line:84%
and their system
continuously gets better.

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:30.870 align:middle line:84%
There are a couple of problems
with doing that in education.

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.450 align:middle line:84%
The first problem is,
if you go to folks

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:33.740 align:middle line:84%
and you say that
what you want to do

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:35.190 align:middle line:84%
is experiment with
their children,

00:43:35.190 --> 00:43:37.580 align:middle line:84%
as we've joked before, people
are not that enthusiastic

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:39.290 align:middle line:90%
about that as an idea.

00:43:39.290 --> 00:43:44.870 align:middle line:84%
It's also not clear that
surveilling people constantly

00:43:44.870 --> 00:43:48.560 align:middle line:84%
in public schools is a good
idea for civil society.

00:43:48.560 --> 00:43:50.540 align:middle line:84%
An exercise maybe
we'll do later on

00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:54.300 align:middle line:84%
is start thinking about all of
the data that MIT has about you.

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:55.830 align:middle line:84%
And we might start
thinking, is it

00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:58.630 align:middle line:84%
a good idea that MIT has all
of this data about people?

00:43:58.630 --> 00:44:00.420 align:middle line:84%
Is it a good idea for
an elementary school

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:03.292 align:middle line:84%
to have all of this data
about where people are moving,

00:44:03.292 --> 00:44:05.250 align:middle line:84%
where they're logging
in, how they're learning,

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:07.470 align:middle line:84%
their health care, all
these other kinds of things?

00:44:07.470 --> 00:44:10.320 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that collecting
huge amounts of data

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:12.190 align:middle line:84%
lets us build really
cool EdTech products.

00:44:12.190 --> 00:44:14.640 align:middle line:84%
It also could be that
it inducts people

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:16.890 align:middle line:84%
into a surveillance society,
which ultimately is not

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:20.700 align:middle line:84%
good for democracies
or civil society.

00:44:20.700 --> 00:44:22.350 align:middle line:84%
So in the second
half of the class,

00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:24.520 align:middle line:84%
or the second half of
our reading of this book,

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:27.000 align:middle line:84%
those are the four
dilemmas that we'll

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:30.330 align:middle line:84%
spend some time messing
around with and exploring.

00:44:30.330 --> 00:44:34.860 align:middle line:84%
That is an overview of the
task of the reading together.

00:44:34.860 --> 00:44:39.030 align:middle line:84%
And then what we'll do for
the last 20 minutes of class

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:42.580 align:middle line:84%
is to talk about massive open
online courses in particular.

00:44:42.580 --> 00:44:50.220 align:middle line:84%
But maybe I'll pause back here
essentially on these two slides

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:53.200 align:middle line:84%
and see if people have any
questions or things they

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:54.360 align:middle line:90%
want to interject here.

00:44:54.360 --> 00:44:58.600 align:middle line:90%


00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:00.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: For the
next slide, when

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:04.300 align:middle line:84%
you mentioned the four as-yet
intractable dilemmas, when

00:45:04.300 --> 00:45:07.510 align:middle line:84%
you were studying technology
and education, have

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:12.130 align:middle line:84%
you found issues that used to be
dilemmas and that at some point

00:45:12.130 --> 00:45:14.800 align:middle line:84%
we were actually able
to solve and these

00:45:14.800 --> 00:45:16.350 align:middle line:90%
are the four that are left?

00:45:16.350 --> 00:45:17.270 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Huh.

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:17.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:17.770 --> 00:45:22.780 align:middle line:84%
So I think there would be
some-- there would be access

00:45:22.780 --> 00:45:25.510 align:middle line:84%
and distribution problems that
you might have thought about

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:29.480 align:middle line:84%
as being really central to lots
of different places 20 years ago

00:45:29.480 --> 00:45:30.790 align:middle line:90%
that would still--

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:32.500 align:middle line:84%
if we're talking about
sub-Saharan Africa

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:36.574 align:middle line:84%
or other kinds of places in
the world are still serious,

00:45:36.574 --> 00:45:39.280 align:middle line:84%
refugee camps, places
of protracted conflicts,

00:45:39.280 --> 00:45:41.165 align:middle line:84%
lots of places where
access is hard.

00:45:41.165 --> 00:45:43.540 align:middle line:84%
But there's also lots of places
where we'd be like, yeah,

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:45.457 align:middle line:84%
in the United States,
pretty much every school

00:45:45.457 --> 00:45:46.790 align:middle line:90%
has high-speed internet now.

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:49.478 align:middle line:84%
And so whereas-- we still have
to think about how we do that

00:45:49.478 --> 00:45:51.270 align:middle line:84%
and how we upgrade it
and things like that.

00:45:51.270 --> 00:45:56.120 align:middle line:84%
But that is a dilemma which
has diminished quite a bit

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:56.760 align:middle line:90%
over time.

00:45:56.760 --> 00:46:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And for sure, some of these
dilemmas we've made progress on.

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.440 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I would particularly say
the Trap of Routine Assessment

00:46:03.440 --> 00:46:07.050 align:middle line:84%
is one where, in the
last 10 or 15 years,

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:11.180 align:middle line:84%
there are definitely places
where you could say, hey,

00:46:11.180 --> 00:46:13.670 align:middle line:84%
we're doing better
automated assessment

00:46:13.670 --> 00:46:15.540 align:middle line:84%
now than we were before
at different levels.

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:17.990 align:middle line:84%
So, I mean, computer
science is probably

00:46:17.990 --> 00:46:21.140 align:middle line:84%
the place where we have the
most robust suite of autograding

00:46:21.140 --> 00:46:22.220 align:middle line:90%
tools.

00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:23.960 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't cover
everything that's

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:26.245 align:middle line:84%
important in computer
science, but boy, you

00:46:26.245 --> 00:46:27.620 align:middle line:84%
can automatically
evaluate people

00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:29.900 align:middle line:90%
on a lot of stuff that matters.

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:30.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:30.410 --> 00:46:34.760 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there's an example
in the book of autograding

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:37.980 align:middle line:84%
spoken language for
language learners,

00:46:37.980 --> 00:46:39.950 align:middle line:84%
that you can talk
out loud and Duolingo

00:46:39.950 --> 00:46:42.533 align:middle line:84%
can tell whether or not you've
pronounced something correctly.

00:46:42.533 --> 00:46:44.420 align:middle line:90%
That's pretty rad.

00:46:44.420 --> 00:46:46.917 align:middle line:90%
And then, I think--

00:46:46.917 --> 00:46:49.000 align:middle line:84%
it'll be interesting to
see in the next few years,

00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:52.410 align:middle line:84%
but my hunch is, large language
models will play an increasingly

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:55.350 align:middle line:84%
large role in at least giving
people meaningful feedback

00:46:55.350 --> 00:46:57.060 align:middle line:90%
on their written products.

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:59.550 align:middle line:84%
Whether or not we
actually grade with them,

00:46:59.550 --> 00:47:02.887 align:middle line:90%
that may be a bridge too far.

00:47:02.887 --> 00:47:04.720 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure we'll use it
in some circumstances.

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:08.070 align:middle line:84%
But I bet there'll be many, many
circumstances-- if you told me,

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:11.410 align:middle line:84%
10 years from now, every time
a student turned in a paper,

00:47:11.410 --> 00:47:13.663 align:middle line:84%
they ran it through an
advanced version of something

00:47:13.663 --> 00:47:15.330 align:middle line:84%
like Grammarly, which
gave them not only

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:17.170 align:middle line:84%
feedback about their
syntax, but was like,

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:19.000 align:middle line:84%
hey, I don't think you have
any evidence in this paragraph,

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:20.400 align:middle line:84%
you might want to go back
and look at that, that

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:21.930 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't surprise me at all.

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:23.740 align:middle line:84%
And that could help
students a lot.

00:47:23.740 --> 00:47:27.390 align:middle line:84%
If there's someone who can-- if
there's an automated bot that

00:47:27.390 --> 00:47:31.030 align:middle line:84%
can give you reasonable feedback
generically about your writing,

00:47:31.030 --> 00:47:32.642 align:middle line:90%
that would be cool.

00:47:32.642 --> 00:47:35.260 align:middle line:84%
I'd rather focus on other
things than giving you feedback.

00:47:35.260 --> 00:47:37.718 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I remember you
mentioning in one of the slides

00:47:37.718 --> 00:47:40.590 align:middle line:84%
that computers are not
very good at things that

00:47:40.590 --> 00:47:44.550 align:middle line:84%
don't have as much structure,
but with things like LLMs,

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:49.030 align:middle line:84%
do you think that
that's going to change?

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:52.000 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: They
will get better.

00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:54.410 align:middle line:90%
I think there is still--

00:47:54.410 --> 00:47:56.560 align:middle line:84%
I think even some of
our earliest evidence

00:47:56.560 --> 00:47:59.920 align:middle line:84%
suggests that these are
really thorny problems.

00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:02.470 align:middle line:84%
So I feel like we're
starting to get

00:48:02.470 --> 00:48:04.780 align:middle line:84%
some feedback about,
for instance, people

00:48:04.780 --> 00:48:08.110 align:middle line:84%
using LLMs to help
them in coding.

00:48:08.110 --> 00:48:09.880 align:middle line:84%
And there's a joke,
which is something

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:16.690 align:middle line:84%
like, using Copilot's encoding
will reduce your initial coding

00:48:16.690 --> 00:48:19.900 align:middle line:84%
time in half, and then it will
double your quality assurance

00:48:19.900 --> 00:48:25.188 align:middle line:84%
time, basically because not only
will it maybe introduce errors,

00:48:25.188 --> 00:48:26.980 align:middle line:84%
but also the people
engineering things just

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:29.060 align:middle line:84%
won't understand what
the bot is doing.

00:48:29.060 --> 00:48:32.320 align:middle line:84%
And the bot is much better
at very specific parts

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:35.090 align:middle line:84%
of building code than it is
in engineering a whole system.

00:48:35.090 --> 00:48:37.245 align:middle line:84%
And so it makes a
bunch of decisions

00:48:37.245 --> 00:48:38.870 align:middle line:84%
that make sense for
a particular thing,

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:40.370 align:middle line:84%
but don't make sense for
building a whole system.

00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:41.950 align:middle line:84%
And then somebody has
to go back and repair

00:48:41.950 --> 00:48:42.990 align:middle line:90%
those kinds of things.

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:45.580 align:middle line:90%


00:48:45.580 --> 00:48:47.620 align:middle line:90%
So in terms of--

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:48.227 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:48:48.227 --> 00:48:50.810 align:middle line:84%
We'll get into this more when
we talk about autograders later.

00:48:50.810 --> 00:48:53.360 align:middle line:84%
But Hal Abelson, who's been
on the CS faculty forever,

00:48:53.360 --> 00:48:55.750 align:middle line:84%
has this wonderful quotation
in one of his earliest books

00:48:55.750 --> 00:48:57.670 align:middle line:84%
on computer science,
which says something

00:48:57.670 --> 00:49:05.020 align:middle line:84%
like, computer programming is
a language for communicating

00:49:05.020 --> 00:49:07.720 align:middle line:84%
between two people about methods
that's only incidentally meant

00:49:07.720 --> 00:49:09.670 align:middle line:90%
to be run by a machine.

00:49:09.670 --> 00:49:12.430 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is that really
good code is actually

00:49:12.430 --> 00:49:16.510 align:middle line:84%
talking amongst people and
not talking to the machine.

00:49:16.510 --> 00:49:20.230 align:middle line:84%
Our autograders are really good
at figuring out whether or not

00:49:20.230 --> 00:49:22.660 align:middle line:84%
code does things and
machines understand,

00:49:22.660 --> 00:49:24.160 align:middle line:84%
and autograders for
computer science

00:49:24.160 --> 00:49:26.590 align:middle line:84%
are not very good at
evaluating things like,

00:49:26.590 --> 00:49:29.530 align:middle line:84%
How well is this code
designed for collaboration

00:49:29.530 --> 00:49:30.350 align:middle line:90%
amongst people?

00:49:30.350 --> 00:49:33.480 align:middle line:84%
How sensibly are the functions
named? and other kinds of things

00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:33.980 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:49:33.980 --> 00:49:36.640 align:middle line:84%
My hunch is, we'll
make progress on it.

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:40.060 align:middle line:84%
My other hunch is that it will
remain a pretty hard problem

00:49:40.060 --> 00:49:42.310 align:middle line:90%
for a pretty long time.

00:49:42.310 --> 00:49:44.930 align:middle line:84%
And also, it's a problem
we'll solve in degrees.

00:49:44.930 --> 00:49:47.870 align:middle line:84%
Will we give better
automatic-- five years

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:50.240 align:middle line:84%
from now, will we give
better automatic feedback

00:49:50.240 --> 00:49:52.280 align:middle line:84%
to the typical middle
school essay written

00:49:52.280 --> 00:49:55.010 align:middle line:84%
in almost any topic, and will
some of that feedback actually

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:55.830 align:middle line:90%
be useful?

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:57.620 align:middle line:90%
It seems almost certain to me.

00:49:57.620 --> 00:49:59.122 align:middle line:84%
Will we be able
to actually grade?

00:49:59.122 --> 00:50:00.830 align:middle line:84%
Will we be able to
actually say something

00:50:00.830 --> 00:50:04.850 align:middle line:84%
like, this paper is better
than this other paper,

00:50:04.850 --> 00:50:08.090 align:middle line:84%
with all the consequences
that are attendant to that?

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:10.628 align:middle line:84%
We already do that to some
extent in the education system,

00:50:10.628 --> 00:50:12.170 align:middle line:84%
but I think we're
still going to find

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:14.030 align:middle line:84%
that we're not confident
in the assessment

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:15.560 align:middle line:90%
that these machines make.

00:50:15.560 --> 00:50:18.450 align:middle line:84%
But fortunately, these are
all testable hypotheses.

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:21.570 align:middle line:84%
So if your predictions were
more optimistic than mine,

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:24.050 align:middle line:90%
you could very well be right.

00:50:24.050 --> 00:50:27.580 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk about massive open
online courses for a bit.

00:50:27.580 --> 00:50:30.320 align:middle line:90%


00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:35.100 align:middle line:84%
MOOCs burst on the scene
in particular 2011, 2012.

00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:36.360 align:middle line:90%
So almost 15 years ago now.

00:50:36.360 --> 00:50:38.998 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have taken a
massive open online course?

00:50:38.998 --> 00:50:40.790 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have
signed up for a class?

00:50:40.790 --> 00:50:42.670 align:middle line:84%
How many of you
signed up for one

00:50:42.670 --> 00:50:44.560 align:middle line:84%
and finished it all
the way through?

00:50:44.560 --> 00:50:45.200 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:45.200 --> 00:50:48.370 align:middle line:84%
So people laugh because that is
a much smaller number of folks.

00:50:48.370 --> 00:50:53.770 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the distinctive
features of online learning,

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:57.610 align:middle line:84%
is that online learning
is really hard.

00:50:57.610 --> 00:51:00.220 align:middle line:84%
If you were not alive
or paying attention

00:51:00.220 --> 00:51:02.900 align:middle line:84%
to education in
2011, 2012, 2013,

00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:07.900 align:middle line:84%
it is hard to understand
how much people assumed

00:51:07.900 --> 00:51:09.850 align:middle line:84%
that these tools
would completely

00:51:09.850 --> 00:51:11.920 align:middle line:90%
change higher education.

00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:16.360 align:middle line:84%
Not be a little bit useful
maybe, but utterly rearrange

00:51:16.360 --> 00:51:17.320 align:middle line:90%
educational systems.

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:18.820 align:middle line:84%
People thought there
was going to be

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:20.240 align:middle line:90%
before MOOCs and after MOOCs.

00:51:20.240 --> 00:51:23.410 align:middle line:84%
Part of the hype of this, the
first really large-scale class

00:51:23.410 --> 00:51:25.997 align:middle line:84%
like this, Sebastian
Thrun and Peter Norvig,

00:51:25.997 --> 00:51:27.080 align:middle line:90%
who are computer science--

00:51:27.080 --> 00:51:28.940 align:middle line:84%
Sebastian Thrun works
on self-driving cars.

00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:30.350 align:middle line:84%
He founded a company
called Udacity.

00:51:30.350 --> 00:51:31.760 align:middle line:84%
Peter Norvig worked
at Google forever.

00:51:31.760 --> 00:51:33.400 align:middle line:84%
They were both
professors at Stanford.

00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:35.650 align:middle line:84%
They created a class
called Intro to AI.

00:51:35.650 --> 00:51:39.650 align:middle line:84%
And they sent an email around
to the IEEE mailing list saying,

00:51:39.650 --> 00:51:41.800 align:middle line:84%
anybody want to take
this Intro to AI class?

00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:44.230 align:middle line:84%
They were particularly
inspired by Sal Khan.

00:51:44.230 --> 00:51:45.040 align:middle line:90%
I think it was--

00:51:45.040 --> 00:51:48.700 align:middle line:84%
who was talking about
the MITx practice sets

00:51:48.700 --> 00:51:50.050 align:middle line:90%
that you were going through?

00:51:50.050 --> 00:51:52.220 align:middle line:84%
They were very
taken by that model.

00:51:52.220 --> 00:51:53.660 align:middle line:84%
You watch a little
bit of a video,

00:51:53.660 --> 00:51:54.780 align:middle line:84%
you solve a couple
of problems, you

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:57.363 align:middle line:84%
watch a little bit more video,
you solve a couple of problems,

00:51:57.363 --> 00:51:59.410 align:middle line:90%
interleave them along.

00:51:59.410 --> 00:52:00.757 align:middle line:90%
They send this email around.

00:52:00.757 --> 00:52:02.590 align:middle line:84%
They're thinking, maybe
a few hundred, maybe

00:52:02.590 --> 00:52:03.840 align:middle line:90%
a few thousand people show up.

00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:05.750 align:middle line:84%
100,000 people sign
up for this course.

00:52:05.750 --> 00:52:10.120 align:middle line:84%
100,000 people, multiples
more than Peter and Sebastian

00:52:10.120 --> 00:52:12.820 align:middle line:84%
combined would have ever
taught on Stanford campus

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:15.070 align:middle line:90%
in their entire career there.

00:52:15.070 --> 00:52:18.040 align:middle line:84%
And there was a
vision like, wow, this

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:20.170 align:middle line:84%
could happen for
everything everywhere.

00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:24.070 align:middle line:84%
This could be all of higher
education in the future.

00:52:24.070 --> 00:52:28.300 align:middle line:84%
The amount of resources that
ultimately people like Peter

00:52:28.300 --> 00:52:31.600 align:middle line:84%
and Sebastian could put behind
a course for 100,000 people,

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:35.540 align:middle line:84%
you could easily spend $100,000
building that course material,

00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:37.820 align:middle line:84%
and then the cost of
every marginal student,

00:52:37.820 --> 00:52:40.050 align:middle line:84%
every additional student
would be quite low.

00:52:40.050 --> 00:52:42.970 align:middle line:84%
So most people who are teaching
a class, you hire someone.

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:45.730 align:middle line:84%
You pay them $60,000 a year in
a community college, maybe less.

00:52:45.730 --> 00:52:47.772 align:middle line:84%
You pay them $100,000 a
year at an elite college,

00:52:47.772 --> 00:52:48.627 align:middle line:90%
maybe a little more.

00:52:48.627 --> 00:52:50.710 align:middle line:84%
And they spend some of
that money making a course,

00:52:50.710 --> 00:52:52.560 align:middle line:84%
and 20 people take
it, and that's that.

00:52:52.560 --> 00:52:54.720 align:middle line:84%
But imagine if you could
concentrate resources

00:52:54.720 --> 00:52:57.810 align:middle line:84%
to make the best possible
instructional sequence, the best

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:58.830 align:middle line:90%
course ever.

00:52:58.830 --> 00:53:02.130 align:middle line:84%
I did my undergraduate at
the University of Virginia.

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:03.960 align:middle line:84%
And the University of
Virginia very briefly

00:53:03.960 --> 00:53:06.310 align:middle line:84%
fired their president
in, I think,

00:53:06.310 --> 00:53:09.810 align:middle line:84%
2013, because she was not
transitioning fast enough

00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:11.790 align:middle line:90%
to this new online world.

00:53:11.790 --> 00:53:17.520 align:middle line:84%
And then the Board of Governors
underestimated her popularity

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:19.350 align:middle line:84%
on campus, and all the
faculty and students

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:22.320 align:middle line:84%
came out on the lawn and
protested, and she got rehired.

00:53:22.320 --> 00:53:29.220 align:middle line:84%
But imagine if MIT's
president, UMass' president got

00:53:29.220 --> 00:53:31.020 align:middle line:84%
fired this week
because they weren't

00:53:31.020 --> 00:53:32.500 align:middle line:90%
moving fast enough on AI.

00:53:32.500 --> 00:53:36.580 align:middle line:84%
That was the equivalent of
what was happening in 2013.

00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:38.060 align:middle line:90%
It was just madness.

00:53:38.060 --> 00:53:40.700 align:middle line:84%
And I had just finished
my PhD, so I had just

00:53:40.700 --> 00:53:43.118 align:middle line:90%
finished at Harvard in 2012.

00:53:43.118 --> 00:53:45.410 align:middle line:84%
I spent a year working at an
organization nearby called

00:53:45.410 --> 00:53:48.080 align:middle line:84%
Facing History & Ourselves,
and I got hired at Harvard

00:53:48.080 --> 00:53:50.570 align:middle line:84%
as the first researcher
whose responsibility

00:53:50.570 --> 00:53:54.620 align:middle line:84%
was to create a research
infrastructure for HarvardX.

00:53:54.620 --> 00:53:57.410 align:middle line:84%
There were three
big bets, that when

00:53:57.410 --> 00:54:02.840 align:middle line:84%
Harvard and MIT came together
to create MOOCs, to create edX--

00:54:02.840 --> 00:54:04.820 align:middle line:84%
MOOCs had been created
in other places, too.

00:54:04.820 --> 00:54:06.810 align:middle line:84%
Coursera at Stanford,
most notably--

00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:08.430 align:middle line:84%
there were three things that
they're going to try to do.

00:54:08.430 --> 00:54:10.370 align:middle line:84%
They're going to provide new
pathways into higher education

00:54:10.370 --> 00:54:12.740 align:middle line:84%
for people who had limited
access, that basically there

00:54:12.740 --> 00:54:14.990 align:middle line:84%
are lots of places in the
world where people can't get

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:16.700 align:middle line:84%
into higher education
because they can't afford it,

00:54:16.700 --> 00:54:19.350 align:middle line:84%
because it's not nearby, the
programs they want aren't there.

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:21.865 align:middle line:84%
If all of that is online, then
as soon as you can connect

00:54:21.865 --> 00:54:23.240 align:middle line:84%
people to the
internet, everybody

00:54:23.240 --> 00:54:26.000 align:middle line:90%
can participate in everything.

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:29.010 align:middle line:84%
People thought that it
would reorganize, disrupt,

00:54:29.010 --> 00:54:31.957 align:middle line:84%
unbundle, profoundly change
systems of higher education.

00:54:31.957 --> 00:54:33.540 align:middle line:84%
So universities might
still be around,

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:36.228 align:middle line:84%
but they could be organized
in totally new ways.

00:54:36.228 --> 00:54:37.770 align:middle line:84%
We were talking just
at the beginning

00:54:37.770 --> 00:54:42.360 align:middle line:84%
about how research universities
often hire people who are not

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:43.890 align:middle line:90%
particularly good at teaching.

00:54:43.890 --> 00:54:45.690 align:middle line:84%
Well, what if all
the teaching just

00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:48.990 align:middle line:84%
got provided by these companies
that specialized in generating

00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:51.883 align:middle line:84%
learning materials, and then
your research professors could

00:54:51.883 --> 00:54:53.550 align:middle line:84%
just go do their
research and all of you

00:54:53.550 --> 00:54:56.320 align:middle line:84%
could take 6.001 from
an online course?

00:54:56.320 --> 00:54:58.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Wasn't there a quote
from the book that was like,

00:54:58.780 --> 00:55:02.760 align:middle line:84%
in 30 years, there would
only be 10 universities left

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:03.580 align:middle line:90%
in the country?

00:55:03.580 --> 00:55:05.140 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And Udacity
could be one of them.

00:55:05.140 --> 00:55:06.682 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, yeah, that
was Sebastian Thrun.

00:55:06.682 --> 00:55:09.013 align:middle line:84%
And the idea probably from
that-- it was probably not

00:55:09.013 --> 00:55:10.180 align:middle line:90%
quite as crazy as it seemed.

00:55:10.180 --> 00:55:11.940 align:middle line:84%
It was probably not
that there would not

00:55:11.940 --> 00:55:13.630 align:middle line:84%
be institutions of
higher education,

00:55:13.630 --> 00:55:17.010 align:middle line:84%
but you would go to UMass or
Bunker Hill Community College

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:20.700 align:middle line:84%
and all of the classes being
taught there would have been

00:55:20.700 --> 00:55:29.183 align:middle line:84%
made at Tsinghua University,
Stanford, Harvard, MIT, CMU,

00:55:29.183 --> 00:55:31.350 align:middle line:84%
and a handful of other
places, Udacity and a handful

00:55:31.350 --> 00:55:32.170 align:middle line:90%
of other places.

00:55:32.170 --> 00:55:35.270 align:middle line:84%
And going to something like
UMass or Bunker Hill Community

00:55:35.270 --> 00:55:38.300 align:middle line:84%
College would mean taking
online courses that

00:55:38.300 --> 00:55:41.070 align:middle line:84%
were created by elite
institutions elsewhere.

00:55:41.070 --> 00:55:44.300 align:middle line:84%
And you'd get a dorm room,
and be in a fraternity,

00:55:44.300 --> 00:55:47.930 align:middle line:84%
and be able to have a human
do recitations and extra help

00:55:47.930 --> 00:55:49.100 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:52.080 align:middle line:84%
But people really--
it sounds ridiculous,

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:53.580 align:middle line:84%
but people absolutely
believed that.

00:55:53.580 --> 00:55:57.440 align:middle line:84%
People thought, for sure, that
higher education would be--

00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:00.740 align:middle line:84%
if we went back into 2012 and
said that higher education is

00:56:00.740 --> 00:56:02.570 align:middle line:84%
basically the
same, except having

00:56:02.570 --> 00:56:06.620 align:middle line:84%
more financial difficulties,
in 2024 than it was in 2012,

00:56:06.620 --> 00:56:09.380 align:middle line:84%
there would be lots of people
who would be very surprised

00:56:09.380 --> 00:56:12.680 align:middle line:90%
by that.

00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:15.527 align:middle line:90%
So I guess this presentation--

00:56:15.527 --> 00:56:18.110 align:middle line:84%
I don't know when I developed
this, probably 2019 or something

00:56:18.110 --> 00:56:19.730 align:middle line:84%
like that, but it
was at the moment

00:56:19.730 --> 00:56:21.860 align:middle line:84%
where it was pretty clear
that none of these bets

00:56:21.860 --> 00:56:25.550 align:middle line:84%
were going to pan out,
that we were not going

00:56:25.550 --> 00:56:26.940 align:middle line:90%
to accomplish these things.

00:56:26.940 --> 00:56:29.680 align:middle line:84%
And I was trying to
help a community--

00:56:29.680 --> 00:56:31.430 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, sometimes
you'd be talking--

00:56:31.430 --> 00:56:33.472 align:middle line:84%
like the people we talked
to last time, education

00:56:33.472 --> 00:56:35.150 align:middle line:84%
deans and stuff like
that, or provosts

00:56:35.150 --> 00:56:39.240 align:middle line:84%
or funders or people
educated in education,

00:56:39.240 --> 00:56:42.770 align:middle line:84%
why didn't MOOCs do the things
that we wanted them to do?

00:56:42.770 --> 00:56:43.645 align:middle line:90%
Oh, first of all--

00:56:43.645 --> 00:56:45.770 align:middle line:84%
actually, I don't have to
spend too much time on it

00:56:45.770 --> 00:56:47.930 align:middle line:84%
because it sounds like
everybody has taken a MOOC.

00:56:47.930 --> 00:56:50.570 align:middle line:84%
But the most important thing
that you need-- the two most

00:56:50.570 --> 00:56:52.880 align:middle line:84%
important buttons in a MOOC
are the Previous button

00:56:52.880 --> 00:56:53.813 align:middle line:90%
and the Next button.

00:56:53.813 --> 00:56:55.730 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of what makes
it instructor-guided

00:56:55.730 --> 00:56:56.720 align:middle line:90%
learning at scale.

00:56:56.720 --> 00:56:58.928 align:middle line:84%
There's a bunch of stuff
that we shove in the middle.

00:56:58.928 --> 00:57:00.900 align:middle line:84%
And then you click
Next, you do some stuff.

00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:03.390 align:middle line:84%
If you get it wrong or get
confused, you click Previous,

00:57:03.390 --> 00:57:06.950 align:middle line:84%
but you linearly make your way
through some instructor-guided

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:08.160 align:middle line:90%
set of materials.

00:57:08.160 --> 00:57:12.288 align:middle line:84%
A bunch of it is video or
animations or text explanation

00:57:12.288 --> 00:57:13.080 align:middle line:90%
or stuff like that.

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:15.455 align:middle line:84%
And then periodically there
are some assessment questions

00:57:15.455 --> 00:57:17.870 align:middle line:90%
that come along.

00:57:17.870 --> 00:57:20.930 align:middle line:84%
We realized, as
education researchers,

00:57:20.930 --> 00:57:27.560 align:middle line:84%
almost right away that folks who
are furthest from opportunity

00:57:27.560 --> 00:57:31.010 align:middle line:84%
were not the ones who are
signing up for MOOCs first.

00:57:31.010 --> 00:57:33.330 align:middle line:84%
This is a paper that was
published in 2019 in Nature

00:57:33.330 --> 00:57:35.100 align:middle line:84%
by Ezekiel Emanuel,
who's actually

00:57:35.100 --> 00:57:37.020 align:middle line:90%
mostly a medical researcher.

00:57:37.020 --> 00:57:39.367 align:middle line:84%
But he was like, in
the United States,

00:57:39.367 --> 00:57:41.200 align:middle line:84%
here's the population
with a college degree.

00:57:41.200 --> 00:57:42.617 align:middle line:84%
Here's the MOOC
student population

00:57:42.617 --> 00:57:43.770 align:middle line:90%
with a college degree.

00:57:43.770 --> 00:57:46.300 align:middle line:84%
In Brazil, Russia, India,
China, South Africa,

00:57:46.300 --> 00:57:48.400 align:middle line:84%
here's the population
with a college degree.

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:50.340 align:middle line:84%
Here's the percent of people
from those countries taking

00:57:50.340 --> 00:57:51.673 align:middle line:90%
MOOCs who have a college degree.

00:57:51.673 --> 00:57:54.360 align:middle line:84%
So basically, we could
tell almost right away

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:57.210 align:middle line:84%
that the people that we
were recruiting into MOOCs

00:57:57.210 --> 00:58:02.190 align:middle line:84%
were folks who were
already educated.

00:58:02.190 --> 00:58:05.370 align:middle line:84%
Today, I don't know
that we know everything

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:07.470 align:middle line:90%
about why that's the case.

00:58:07.470 --> 00:58:09.390 align:middle line:84%
People who have
earned an education

00:58:09.390 --> 00:58:12.180 align:middle line:84%
probably enjoy education
as leisure time activities

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:14.410 align:middle line:84%
more than people who
don't pursue an education.

00:58:14.410 --> 00:58:15.872 align:middle line:90%
That would be normal.

00:58:15.872 --> 00:58:17.580 align:middle line:84%
But I would say that
one thing that we've

00:58:17.580 --> 00:58:20.790 align:middle line:84%
discovered in various formats is
that online, self-paced learning

00:58:20.790 --> 00:58:24.040 align:middle line:84%
is incredibly
difficult for people.

00:58:24.040 --> 00:58:26.402 align:middle line:84%
You all laughingly joked
about that when I said,

00:58:26.402 --> 00:58:28.860 align:middle line:84%
how many of you have started
a MOOC that you didn't finish?

00:58:28.860 --> 00:58:31.090 align:middle line:84%
And you all are some of the
best people in the world

00:58:31.090 --> 00:58:32.210 align:middle line:90%
at self-paced learning.

00:58:32.210 --> 00:58:33.490 align:middle line:90%
And you found it hard.

00:58:33.490 --> 00:58:36.040 align:middle line:84%
The entire population, the
vast majority of people

00:58:36.040 --> 00:58:39.340 align:middle line:84%
find online self-paced learning
really, really difficult.

00:58:39.340 --> 00:58:41.630 align:middle line:84%
They have other
priorities in their life.

00:58:41.630 --> 00:58:42.452 align:middle line:90%
It's boring.

00:58:42.452 --> 00:58:44.410 align:middle line:84%
And actually, an interesting
thing about people

00:58:44.410 --> 00:58:46.035 align:middle line:84%
is that when they're
studying something

00:58:46.035 --> 00:58:49.370 align:middle line:84%
they really care about, they can
be quite persistent in learning.

00:58:49.370 --> 00:58:53.230 align:middle line:84%
So you study young people
who are like-- right now,

00:58:53.230 --> 00:58:58.930 align:middle line:84%
if you ask young tweens/teens
to explore skincare routines

00:58:58.930 --> 00:59:02.750 align:middle line:84%
online, they will pursue
that with great passion,

00:59:02.750 --> 00:59:05.500 align:middle line:84%
great determination, persist
through lots of difficulties,

00:59:05.500 --> 00:59:06.820 align:middle line:90%
and be successful.

00:59:06.820 --> 00:59:09.030 align:middle line:84%
They will not do the
same with algebra.

00:59:09.030 --> 00:59:11.530 align:middle line:84%
You kind of wish as a society
that they were better doing it

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:12.950 align:middle line:84%
in algebra than
skincare routines,

00:59:12.950 --> 00:59:15.430 align:middle line:84%
but that's just not the way
human beings seem to be wired.

00:59:15.430 --> 00:59:19.870 align:middle line:84%
So who gets good at online
self-paced learning?

00:59:19.870 --> 00:59:23.260 align:middle line:84%
How do you become good at
being a good autodidact?

00:59:23.260 --> 00:59:25.127 align:middle line:84%
I think the best
guess we have is

00:59:25.127 --> 00:59:27.710 align:middle line:84%
that you get an apprenticeship
in the formal education system.

00:59:27.710 --> 00:59:29.950 align:middle line:84%
The best way to be good
at learning on your own

00:59:29.950 --> 00:59:31.960 align:middle line:84%
is to learn with a
bunch of other people.

00:59:31.960 --> 00:59:34.810 align:middle line:84%
So that means the way to
become really good at taking

00:59:34.810 --> 00:59:37.760 align:middle line:84%
these online, self-paced MOOCs
is to go get a college degree.

00:59:37.760 --> 00:59:41.770 align:middle line:84%
But if your goal was to create
new pathways into higher

00:59:41.770 --> 00:59:44.657 align:middle line:84%
education for limited access,
building a thing which

00:59:44.657 --> 00:59:46.990 align:middle line:84%
you really have to have spent
a lot of time in education

00:59:46.990 --> 00:59:49.660 align:middle line:84%
to be good at is maybe
not the best strategy.

00:59:49.660 --> 00:59:52.390 align:middle line:84%
But people didn't
know that at the time.

00:59:52.390 --> 00:59:55.010 align:middle line:84%
This is an article that I
wrote with Mariano's advisor,

00:59:55.010 --> 00:59:59.560 align:middle line:84%
José, where we were
trying to figure out

00:59:59.560 --> 01:00:01.540 align:middle line:90%
why MOOCs were not working.

01:00:01.540 --> 01:00:04.583 align:middle line:84%
And one of the things
that we observed-- these

01:00:04.583 --> 01:00:07.000 align:middle line:84%
are charts of enrollment, but
they're charts of enrollment

01:00:07.000 --> 01:00:09.530 align:middle line:84%
by human development
index categories.

01:00:09.530 --> 01:00:11.380 align:middle line:84%
So basically, way
more people were

01:00:11.380 --> 01:00:13.445 align:middle line:84%
coming from the world's
most affluent countries

01:00:13.445 --> 01:00:15.070 align:middle line:84%
than were coming from
the world's least

01:00:15.070 --> 01:00:15.950 align:middle line:90%
affluent countries.

01:00:15.950 --> 01:00:18.040 align:middle line:84%
And we basically
made no dent in it

01:00:18.040 --> 01:00:21.080 align:middle line:84%
over the first
eight years or so.

01:00:21.080 --> 01:00:24.440 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine, oh, well, we
started with a pretty big gap.

01:00:24.440 --> 01:00:25.870 align:middle line:84%
If these three
lines in the bottom

01:00:25.870 --> 01:00:28.740 align:middle line:84%
had continued to traverse
up, then maybe you could say,

01:00:28.740 --> 01:00:30.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, we're making progress
towards our goals.

01:00:30.700 --> 01:00:32.450 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that
was kind of a problem

01:00:32.450 --> 01:00:35.180 align:middle line:84%
is, heading into the
pandemic, enrollments

01:00:35.180 --> 01:00:38.070 align:middle line:84%
and if you're trying to run a
company or something like that,

01:00:38.070 --> 01:00:40.140 align:middle line:84%
it's really not good to
have this line go down.

01:00:40.140 --> 01:00:41.057 align:middle line:90%
You'd really like it--

01:00:41.057 --> 01:00:42.890 align:middle line:84%
if this was Facebook's
graph, it would just

01:00:42.890 --> 01:00:45.080 align:middle line:84%
keep going up
exponentially forever.

01:00:45.080 --> 01:00:46.970 align:middle line:84%
That was another
problem with MOOCs

01:00:46.970 --> 01:00:48.993 align:middle line:84%
compared to other
social media businesses.

01:00:48.993 --> 01:00:51.660 align:middle line:84%
When you sign up for Facebook or
Twitter or something like that,

01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:53.360 align:middle line:84%
you're kind of stuck
in there forever.

01:00:53.360 --> 01:00:56.270 align:middle line:84%
When you sign up for a class,
the class ends, and then

01:00:56.270 --> 01:00:59.300 align:middle line:84%
you leave and go do something
else with your life.

01:00:59.300 --> 01:01:01.070 align:middle line:90%
So why didn't this work?

01:01:01.070 --> 01:01:06.147 align:middle line:84%
Or actually, we'll talk
about the second one next.

01:01:06.147 --> 01:01:07.730 align:middle line:84%
But what were you
going to say, Maria?

01:01:07.730 --> 01:01:09.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I was just reflecting.

01:01:09.390 --> 01:01:10.910 align:middle line:84%
One thing that I
realized as well

01:01:10.910 --> 01:01:15.932 align:middle line:84%
recently is that everyone
now creates their own course.

01:01:15.932 --> 01:01:19.170 align:middle line:84%
Every influencer has a course
on something different, right?

01:01:19.170 --> 01:01:22.670 align:middle line:84%
And I think that probably also
affected this ability of MOOCs

01:01:22.670 --> 01:01:27.090 align:middle line:84%
to spread because, originally,
five or six organizations

01:01:27.090 --> 01:01:28.230 align:middle line:90%
were creating content.

01:01:28.230 --> 01:01:31.000 align:middle line:84%
And nowadays, this is
very decentralized.

01:01:31.000 --> 01:01:33.300 align:middle line:84%
So even if it's
still hard for people

01:01:33.300 --> 01:01:36.210 align:middle line:84%
to complete these courses
online, et cetera, et cetera,

01:01:36.210 --> 01:01:39.312 align:middle line:84%
there's also a lot more
competition, right?

01:01:39.312 --> 01:01:41.520 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: There's a lot
of competition out there.

01:01:41.520 --> 01:01:43.650 align:middle line:84%
And traditional
institutions prove

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:45.370 align:middle line:84%
to be very effective
in that competition.

01:01:45.370 --> 01:01:47.340 align:middle line:84%
So here's the thing
which shocked even me.

01:01:47.340 --> 01:01:48.820 align:middle line:84%
At the beginning
of the pandemic--

01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:51.160 align:middle line:84%
you all remember
this from March 2020,

01:01:51.160 --> 01:01:53.640 align:middle line:84%
April 2020-- we
sent you all home.

01:01:53.640 --> 01:01:56.820 align:middle line:84%
And here's something
that could have happened.

01:01:56.820 --> 01:02:00.150 align:middle line:84%
There were thousands of
professors in the United States

01:02:00.150 --> 01:02:03.010 align:middle line:84%
who were teaching Introduction
to Microeconomics.

01:02:03.010 --> 01:02:04.510 align:middle line:84%
And a bunch of them
could have said,

01:02:04.510 --> 01:02:07.110 align:middle line:84%
there is no way I can do a good
job continuing to teach you

01:02:07.110 --> 01:02:08.280 align:middle line:90%
Introduction to Economics.

01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:09.720 align:middle line:84%
My kids aren't in
school, they're

01:02:09.720 --> 01:02:11.350 align:middle line:84%
running underneath
my feet right now.

01:02:11.350 --> 01:02:12.940 align:middle line:90%
I've never taught online before.

01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:17.008 align:middle line:84%
Just go to edX, go to
Johns Hopkins, go to MIT,

01:02:17.008 --> 01:02:19.300 align:middle line:84%
take their courses, and I'll
give you a test at the end

01:02:19.300 --> 01:02:21.060 align:middle line:90%
and help you out.

01:02:21.060 --> 01:02:25.210 align:middle line:84%
The alternative is that, I take
my lectures, I put them online,

01:02:25.210 --> 01:02:26.770 align:middle line:90%
and we meet in Zoom once a week.

01:02:26.770 --> 01:02:28.520 align:middle line:84%
And I still keep giving
you your lectures.

01:02:28.520 --> 01:02:31.300 align:middle line:84%
Basically every learner and
institution in the world

01:02:31.300 --> 01:02:33.790 align:middle line:90%
preferred the latter option.

01:02:33.790 --> 01:02:35.680 align:middle line:84%
All throughout the
pandemic, at all

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:38.180 align:middle line:84%
levels of the education around
the world, we basically said,

01:02:38.180 --> 01:02:41.050 align:middle line:84%
would you rather have this
really high production value

01:02:41.050 --> 01:02:42.755 align:middle line:84%
class kind of
offered anonymously

01:02:42.755 --> 01:02:44.380 align:middle line:84%
from some institution
that maybe you've

01:02:44.380 --> 01:02:45.838 align:middle line:84%
heard of but you
have no connection

01:02:45.838 --> 01:02:49.308 align:middle line:84%
or with your own
harried professor, who's

01:02:49.308 --> 01:02:51.100 align:middle line:84%
taking care of his kids
while teaching you,

01:02:51.100 --> 01:02:53.100 align:middle line:84%
who doesn't know anything
about online learning?

01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:55.420 align:middle line:84%
And people overwhelmingly
picked the latter.

01:02:55.420 --> 01:02:56.602 align:middle line:90%
I don't know why.

01:02:56.602 --> 01:02:58.060 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why,
we don't know why

01:02:58.060 --> 01:02:59.852 align:middle line:84%
they picked the latter,
other than the fact

01:02:59.852 --> 01:03:03.580 align:middle line:84%
that education is a
profoundly social enterprise.

01:03:03.580 --> 01:03:05.715 align:middle line:84%
Even the comment you made
about influencers having

01:03:05.715 --> 01:03:07.090 align:middle line:84%
their own courses
and things like

01:03:07.090 --> 01:03:09.280 align:middle line:84%
that, you have a
parasocial relationship

01:03:09.280 --> 01:03:12.370 align:middle line:84%
with those influencers in a
way that you wouldn't from Josh

01:03:12.370 --> 01:03:14.260 align:middle line:84%
Angrist, who teaches
the economics class here

01:03:14.260 --> 01:03:16.410 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

01:03:16.410 --> 01:03:16.910 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:03:16.910 --> 01:03:19.300 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to say two more
things before we head out.

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:21.133 align:middle line:84%
Oh, well, some of these
things are on there.

01:03:21.133 --> 01:03:23.750 align:middle line:90%


01:03:23.750 --> 01:03:25.580 align:middle line:84%
The main advantage
that MOOCs have

01:03:25.580 --> 01:03:27.630 align:middle line:90%
is they reduce faculty labor.

01:03:27.630 --> 01:03:29.820 align:middle line:84%
They pull faculty
out of the equation.

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:33.200 align:middle line:84%
They just make them television
stars instead of teachers.

01:03:33.200 --> 01:03:37.880 align:middle line:84%
And there's basically no way
to do the economics of MOOCs,

01:03:37.880 --> 01:03:40.730 align:middle line:84%
except to dramatically
reduce human contact.

01:03:40.730 --> 01:03:45.020 align:middle line:84%
But what helps people
learn is human contact.

01:03:45.020 --> 01:03:47.645 align:middle line:84%
The way that you persist in most
of your learning environments,

01:03:47.645 --> 01:03:50.228 align:middle line:84%
especially when you're learning
something you don't care about

01:03:50.228 --> 01:03:51.870 align:middle line:84%
and are not that
interested in, is

01:03:51.870 --> 01:03:53.400 align:middle line:84%
you care what your
faculty thinks about it.

01:03:53.400 --> 01:03:54.650 align:middle line:90%
You care where your peers are.

01:03:54.650 --> 01:03:58.190 align:middle line:84%
You have all these other human
things that keep you connected.

01:03:58.190 --> 01:04:01.040 align:middle line:84%
And because of that,
most people are not

01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:02.580 align:middle line:90%
good at self-regulated learning.

01:04:02.580 --> 01:04:03.680 align:middle line:84%
If the vast majority
of people are not

01:04:03.680 --> 01:04:05.510 align:middle line:84%
good at self-regulated
learning, if the only way

01:04:05.510 --> 01:04:07.177 align:middle line:84%
to get better at
self-regulated learning

01:04:07.177 --> 01:04:09.900 align:middle line:84%
is to go through the K-12
system and go to college,

01:04:09.900 --> 01:04:12.500 align:middle line:84%
then this is not going to be a
good system for helping folks

01:04:12.500 --> 01:04:16.730 align:middle line:84%
further from opportunity
get into college.

01:04:16.730 --> 01:04:19.280 align:middle line:84%
The primary way that MOOCs
were going to disrupt

01:04:19.280 --> 01:04:22.380 align:middle line:90%
higher education is--

01:04:22.380 --> 01:04:25.230 align:middle line:84%
there was a very, for me,
influential article that said,

01:04:25.230 --> 01:04:29.050 align:middle line:84%
the actual innovation-- nothing
was innovative in MOOCs.

01:04:29.050 --> 01:04:31.450 align:middle line:84%
A learning management system
is not new. edX was not new.

01:04:31.450 --> 01:04:32.410 align:middle line:90%
Coursera was not new.

01:04:32.410 --> 01:04:33.760 align:middle line:90%
Canvas was doing the same thing.

01:04:33.760 --> 01:04:34.720 align:middle line:90%
Moodle was doing the same thing.

01:04:34.720 --> 01:04:36.220 align:middle line:84%
Blackboard was doing
the same thing.

01:04:36.220 --> 01:04:37.345 align:middle line:90%
Autograders are not new.

01:04:37.345 --> 01:04:39.720 align:middle line:84%
We had all kinds of autograders
and all kinds of systems,

01:04:39.720 --> 01:04:41.580 align:middle line:84%
that the actual
innovation in MOOCs

01:04:41.580 --> 01:04:44.920 align:middle line:84%
was putting a storefront on top
of a learning management system,

01:04:44.920 --> 01:04:48.730 align:middle line:84%
that the thing that was actually
different was that, in the past,

01:04:48.730 --> 01:04:50.410 align:middle line:84%
if you wanted to buy
a Harvard course,

01:04:50.410 --> 01:04:53.760 align:middle line:84%
you had to buy eight a year, and
you had to rent an apartment,

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:56.220 align:middle line:84%
and you had to buy all your
meals from the institution.

01:04:56.220 --> 01:04:59.250 align:middle line:84%
You had to buy this
giant bundle of stuff.

01:04:59.250 --> 01:05:02.550 align:middle line:84%
And so the bet that Harvard,
MIT, Stanford, other folks made

01:05:02.550 --> 01:05:05.460 align:middle line:84%
is, what if we sold just
one course at a time?

01:05:05.460 --> 01:05:07.620 align:middle line:84%
What if you could
come in and just

01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:11.200 align:middle line:84%
take one Intro to CS class, one
Introduction to Economics class.

01:05:11.200 --> 01:05:16.170 align:middle line:84%
And millions of people
were interested in that.

01:05:16.170 --> 01:05:17.820 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the number
of sign-ups that

01:05:17.820 --> 01:05:19.270 align:middle line:84%
appeared in many
of these courses

01:05:19.270 --> 01:05:21.760 align:middle line:84%
were these astronomically
large numbers.

01:05:21.760 --> 01:05:24.323 align:middle line:84%
CS50 at Harvard had
millions of people enrolled

01:05:24.323 --> 01:05:25.490 align:middle line:90%
with just a couple of years.

01:05:25.490 --> 01:05:27.760 align:middle line:84%
A tiny, tiny fraction
of them finish.

01:05:27.760 --> 01:05:29.480 align:middle line:84%
Something like 1%
of them finish.

01:05:29.480 --> 01:05:33.225 align:middle line:84%
On average, MOOCs, about 5% of
people who register, about 10%

01:05:33.225 --> 01:05:34.600 align:middle line:84%
of people who
click into a course

01:05:34.600 --> 01:05:36.830 align:middle line:90%
once will persist in a class.

01:05:36.830 --> 01:05:39.610 align:middle line:90%
But pretty small numbers.

01:05:39.610 --> 01:05:44.950 align:middle line:84%
However, it turned
out that storefronts

01:05:44.950 --> 01:05:47.510 align:middle line:84%
didn't solve all of the problems
that universities stored.

01:05:47.510 --> 01:05:50.530 align:middle line:84%
So people were excited
to try out storefronts.

01:05:50.530 --> 01:05:53.530 align:middle line:84%
But storefronts by themselves
didn't confer legitimacy.

01:05:53.530 --> 01:05:56.620 align:middle line:84%
Really traditional things
like registrar offices

01:05:56.620 --> 01:05:58.360 align:middle line:90%
confirmed legitimacy.

01:05:58.360 --> 01:06:02.260 align:middle line:84%
Things that publish transcripts
and degrees and diplomas

01:06:02.260 --> 01:06:04.270 align:middle line:90%
confer legitimacy.

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:09.130 align:middle line:84%
And so this idea that MOOCs
could move forward just

01:06:09.130 --> 01:06:13.100 align:middle line:84%
by selling one course
at a time proved untrue.

01:06:13.100 --> 01:06:15.310 align:middle line:84%
So then MOOCs did
something which

01:06:15.310 --> 01:06:17.780 align:middle line:84%
was surprising to people, which
they said, well, all right,

01:06:17.780 --> 01:06:20.380 align:middle line:84%
why don't we start offering
whole degree programs?

01:06:20.380 --> 01:06:23.360 align:middle line:84%
How about instead of taking
just one MOOC at a time,

01:06:23.360 --> 01:06:25.280 align:middle line:84%
you'll take eight
MOOCs and get an MBA?

01:06:25.280 --> 01:06:27.503 align:middle line:84%
You'll take eight MOOCs
and get a data science

01:06:27.503 --> 01:06:29.920 align:middle line:84%
degree, a computer science
degree, or something like that.

01:06:29.920 --> 01:06:32.900 align:middle line:84%
There were companies that had
been doing that for a long time.

01:06:32.900 --> 01:06:35.890 align:middle line:84%
Those companies were called
Online Program Managers.

01:06:35.890 --> 01:06:40.120 align:middle line:84%
They are some of the most
unreputable companies

01:06:40.120 --> 01:06:42.880 align:middle line:84%
in the higher education
space, for a few reasons.

01:06:42.880 --> 01:06:45.760 align:middle line:84%
One is, they tended to
be pretty secretive.

01:06:45.760 --> 01:06:48.640 align:middle line:84%
So you would sign up for
an online degree from

01:06:48.640 --> 01:06:51.400 align:middle line:84%
an organization, and you think
you're getting this degree from

01:06:51.400 --> 01:06:54.850 align:middle line:84%
the University of Chicago,
but you slowly realize that

01:06:54.850 --> 01:06:59.850 align:middle line:84%
there's a company called 2U
or MBANet or Pearson MBANet

01:06:59.850 --> 01:07:02.350 align:middle line:84%
services or something like that,
which is actually providing

01:07:02.350 --> 01:07:04.162 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of your
educational experiences.

01:07:04.162 --> 01:07:06.370 align:middle line:84%
The Wall Street Journal, in
the last couple of years,

01:07:06.370 --> 01:07:08.920 align:middle line:84%
has had a bunch of
exposés of boot camps

01:07:08.920 --> 01:07:10.848 align:middle line:84%
that are branded by
state universities,

01:07:10.848 --> 01:07:12.640 align:middle line:84%
but they're actually
just run by a company.

01:07:12.640 --> 01:07:15.980 align:middle line:84%
It's called the Oregon Computer
Science Boot Camp or whatever,

01:07:15.980 --> 01:07:18.710 align:middle line:84%
Computer Programming Bootcamp,
but nobody who teaches in it

01:07:18.710 --> 01:07:22.760 align:middle line:84%
works for the University of
Oregon, things like that.

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:25.370 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the other problem
that universities faced

01:07:25.370 --> 01:07:29.630 align:middle line:84%
was that a key
precept of businesses

01:07:29.630 --> 01:07:33.385 align:middle line:84%
is that you do not outsource
your core competencies.

01:07:33.385 --> 01:07:34.760 align:middle line:84%
So if you're a
university and you

01:07:34.760 --> 01:07:37.233 align:middle line:84%
outsource your janitorial
services, that is a great idea.

01:07:37.233 --> 01:07:39.900 align:middle line:84%
If you outsource your accounting
services, that is a great idea.

01:07:39.900 --> 01:07:41.940 align:middle line:84%
If you outsource your
teaching and learning,

01:07:41.940 --> 01:07:43.137 align:middle line:90%
that is a bad idea.

01:07:43.137 --> 01:07:44.720 align:middle line:84%
The teaching and
learning is the thing

01:07:44.720 --> 01:07:47.990 align:middle line:90%
that you do as a university.

01:07:47.990 --> 01:07:50.490 align:middle line:90%
So I don't know.

01:07:50.490 --> 01:07:53.720 align:middle line:84%
I wrote this paper
in 2019, which

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:56.670 align:middle line:84%
said something like, man, these
massive open online courses,

01:07:56.670 --> 01:08:00.110 align:middle line:84%
they sure seem to be behaving
like Online Program Managers.

01:08:00.110 --> 01:08:02.242 align:middle line:84%
They're partnering with
these organizations.

01:08:02.242 --> 01:08:04.700 align:middle line:84%
Part of the ways that they were
compelling to organizations

01:08:04.700 --> 01:08:06.840 align:middle line:84%
is that they would go
to a university and say,

01:08:06.840 --> 01:08:10.640 align:middle line:84%
we will pay up front for the
development of these degree

01:08:10.640 --> 01:08:11.400 align:middle line:90%
programs.

01:08:11.400 --> 01:08:13.790 align:middle line:84%
But then, for the
next 15 years, we'll

01:08:13.790 --> 01:08:19.948 align:middle line:84%
take 40%, 50%, 60% of all the
tuition revenue that comes in.

01:08:19.948 --> 01:08:22.343 align:middle line:84%
In year one, that might
seem like a good idea

01:08:22.343 --> 01:08:23.760 align:middle line:84%
because you didn't
spend any money

01:08:23.760 --> 01:08:25.479 align:middle line:84%
and you got this
new tuition revenue.

01:08:25.479 --> 01:08:29.880 align:middle line:84%
But 10 years from now, you're
like, why am I paying 2U,

01:08:29.880 --> 01:08:33.990 align:middle line:84%
Coursera, edX, 60%, 50%, 40%
of all the tuition revenue

01:08:33.990 --> 01:08:36.000 align:middle line:84%
that's coming in when
what they're doing is,

01:08:36.000 --> 01:08:37.470 align:middle line:90%
in some respects, not that hard.

01:08:37.470 --> 01:08:38.970 align:middle line:84%
They're running a
learning management system

01:08:38.970 --> 01:08:40.262 align:middle line:90%
and doing some online learning.

01:08:40.262 --> 01:08:42.090 align:middle line:84%
We can figure out how
to do those things.

01:08:42.090 --> 01:08:43.928 align:middle line:84%
This was a bit of a
controversial paper when

01:08:43.928 --> 01:08:45.720 align:middle line:84%
I published it, and
there was a whole bunch

01:08:45.720 --> 01:08:49.000 align:middle line:84%
of people who said, come on,
Justin, that's really not fair.

01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:52.370 align:middle line:84%
You can't say that MOOCs are
turning into Online Program

01:08:52.370 --> 01:08:52.870 align:middle line:90%
Managers.

01:08:52.870 --> 01:08:56.100 align:middle line:84%
But then two years later, 2U,
who was the largest Online

01:08:56.100 --> 01:08:58.319 align:middle line:90%
Program Manager, bought edX.

01:08:58.319 --> 01:09:04.830 align:middle line:84%
And even that was a convergence
that I wouldn't have projected.

01:09:04.830 --> 01:09:07.470 align:middle line:84%
So I know some of
you may know that all

01:09:07.470 --> 01:09:12.270 align:middle line:84%
of the proceeds
from the sale of edX

01:09:12.270 --> 01:09:15.040 align:middle line:84%
went to this nonprofit
controlled by Harvard and MIT,

01:09:15.040 --> 01:09:16.270 align:middle line:90%
called Axim.

01:09:16.270 --> 01:09:20.859 align:middle line:90%
So 2U paid $800 million for edX.

01:09:20.859 --> 01:09:24.939 align:middle line:84%
And that $800 million went
to start a new nonprofit.

01:09:24.939 --> 01:09:26.990 align:middle line:84%
Headlines like this were
kind of funny to me.

01:09:26.990 --> 01:09:29.620 align:middle line:84%
"Harvard and MIT-led nonprofit
to tackle longstanding

01:09:29.620 --> 01:09:31.420 align:middle line:90%
inequities in education."

01:09:31.420 --> 01:09:34.644 align:middle line:90%
But that's exactly what edX was.

01:09:34.644 --> 01:09:37.350 align:middle line:84%
edX was a nonprofit designed to
tackle inequities in education.

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:38.870 align:middle line:84%
It was like, well,
that didn't work,

01:09:38.870 --> 01:09:41.520 align:middle line:90%
so we're starting a new one.

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:44.020 align:middle line:84%
I just like how the big headline
is like, this is this brand

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:45.145 align:middle line:90%
new thing that we're doing.

01:09:45.145 --> 01:09:48.160 align:middle line:84%
And by the way, it's because we
sold the last version of this

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:49.090 align:middle line:90%
that we just did.

01:09:49.090 --> 01:09:50.340 align:middle line:90%
Tiny font at the bottom.

01:09:50.340 --> 01:09:52.960 align:middle line:90%


01:09:52.960 --> 01:09:56.500 align:middle line:84%
This was last year's
sales price for edX--

01:09:56.500 --> 01:09:57.970 align:middle line:90%
or for 2U.

01:09:57.970 --> 01:10:00.778 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, their stock
has declined 90%

01:10:00.778 --> 01:10:01.820 align:middle line:90%
over the last five years.

01:10:01.820 --> 01:10:03.500 align:middle line:84%
You can see the
pandemic bubble here.

01:10:03.500 --> 01:10:05.420 align:middle line:84%
People did a bunch
of online learning.

01:10:05.420 --> 01:10:08.120 align:middle line:84%
And then partly they were
less enthusiastic about it.

01:10:08.120 --> 01:10:10.570 align:middle line:84%
But also, as universities
get better at offering online

01:10:10.570 --> 01:10:13.460 align:middle line:84%
learning, they don't have to
partner with groups like 2U

01:10:13.460 --> 01:10:14.630 align:middle line:90%
anymore.

01:10:14.630 --> 01:10:19.460 align:middle line:84%
This is actually today's stock
quote, where it is down 99.36%

01:10:19.460 --> 01:10:21.890 align:middle line:90%
over the last five years.

01:10:21.890 --> 01:10:27.260 align:middle line:84%
So 2U paid for edX substantially
more than the entire company

01:10:27.260 --> 01:10:29.240 align:middle line:90%
of 2U is currently worth today.

01:10:29.240 --> 01:10:31.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess I was just
looking for clarification

01:10:31.490 --> 01:10:34.640 align:middle line:90%
on edX and MITx and HarvardX.

01:10:34.640 --> 01:10:37.452 align:middle line:84%
Is that all the same thing,
like the MITx that we use now?

01:10:37.452 --> 01:10:38.160 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: No.

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:38.827 align:middle line:90%
Well, all right.

01:10:38.827 --> 01:10:43.460 align:middle line:84%
So Harvard and MIT
each put $30 million.

01:10:43.460 --> 01:10:47.330 align:middle line:84%
They create units called
HarvardX and MITx.

01:10:47.330 --> 01:10:50.520 align:middle line:90%
Those are independent brands.

01:10:50.520 --> 01:10:53.000 align:middle line:84%
The shared infrastructure
that those things used

01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:54.680 align:middle line:90%
was this thing called edX.

01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:55.920 align:middle line:90%
edX was a company.

01:10:55.920 --> 01:10:58.940 align:middle line:84%
It also created an open source
learning management system

01:10:58.940 --> 01:11:00.020 align:middle line:90%
called Open edX.

01:11:00.020 --> 01:11:06.390 align:middle line:84%
So the nonprofit edX was sold to
2U to become part of a company.

01:11:06.390 --> 01:11:08.540 align:middle line:84%
But there still is an
open source project

01:11:08.540 --> 01:11:12.590 align:middle line:84%
called edX, which is a learning
management system software.

01:11:12.590 --> 01:11:14.330 align:middle line:84%
MITx, and if there's
still a thing

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:16.590 align:middle line:84%
called HarvardX, to some
extent in their products,

01:11:16.590 --> 01:11:19.247 align:middle line:90%
use this thing called Open edX.

01:11:19.247 --> 01:11:20.330 align:middle line:90%
That's what they're doing.

01:11:20.330 --> 01:11:22.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The
infrastructure at MIT.

01:11:22.040 --> 01:11:25.460 align:middle line:84%
The content that we look at
when we go on MITx as students

01:11:25.460 --> 01:11:30.260 align:middle line:90%
is not owned by this company.

01:11:30.260 --> 01:11:32.840 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yes, although
it's slightly more complicated

01:11:32.840 --> 01:11:37.910 align:middle line:84%
than that because I think they
definitely bought the rights

01:11:37.910 --> 01:11:40.255 align:middle line:90%
to display all or most of them.

01:11:40.255 --> 01:11:41.630 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they could
display anything

01:11:41.630 --> 01:11:43.172 align:middle line:84%
that they had
instructors' permission

01:11:43.172 --> 01:11:44.100 align:middle line:90%
to be able to display.

01:11:44.100 --> 01:11:49.100 align:middle line:84%
I took all of my courses off of
edX when it was bought by 2U,

01:11:49.100 --> 01:11:51.810 align:middle line:84%
because I don't want to support
Online Program Managers.

01:11:51.810 --> 01:11:53.810 align:middle line:84%
I don't think they're
good for higher education.

01:11:53.810 --> 01:11:55.657 align:middle line:84%
Some departments did
that, things like that.

01:11:55.657 --> 01:11:57.990 align:middle line:84%
They still might have some
rights to display some of it,

01:11:57.990 --> 01:11:59.940 align:middle line:84%
but they still basically
did what they wanted.

01:11:59.940 --> 01:12:02.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because if you
look at it, a lot of it--

01:12:02.060 --> 01:12:04.320 align:middle line:84%
and even OpenCourseWare
is also from 2008.

01:12:04.320 --> 01:12:06.260 align:middle line:84%
It hasn't been updated
in many years, which

01:12:06.260 --> 01:12:07.260 align:middle line:90%
I guess makes sense.

01:12:07.260 --> 01:12:08.052 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

01:12:08.052 --> 01:12:11.157 align:middle line:84%
And in some ways, in theory,
a lot of the argument

01:12:11.157 --> 01:12:12.490 align:middle line:90%
is that it shouldn't have to be.

01:12:12.490 --> 01:12:18.550 align:middle line:84%
How much did multivariable
calculus change since 2008?

01:12:18.550 --> 01:12:20.130 align:middle line:90%
Not that much.

01:12:20.130 --> 01:12:22.590 align:middle line:84%
And part of the way that you
gain the economy of scale

01:12:22.590 --> 01:12:24.040 align:middle line:84%
is by leaving
these things alone.

01:12:24.040 --> 01:12:24.310 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:12:24.310 --> 01:12:26.350 align:middle line:84%
We'll come back, and
I'll finish this in here,

01:12:26.350 --> 01:12:29.070 align:middle line:84%
except to say that we
tried a whole lot of stuff

01:12:29.070 --> 01:12:31.090 align:middle line:84%
to develop a new
science of learning.

01:12:31.090 --> 01:12:33.030 align:middle line:84%
And probably the main
thing that we learned

01:12:33.030 --> 01:12:35.130 align:middle line:84%
is that people who
click a lot into things

01:12:35.130 --> 01:12:36.630 align:middle line:84%
do better than
people who click less

01:12:36.630 --> 01:12:39.780 align:middle line:84%
into things, which you probably
didn't need a $60 million

01:12:39.780 --> 01:12:41.730 align:middle line:84%
investment to be
able to figure out.

01:12:41.730 --> 01:12:43.630 align:middle line:84%
But I'll finish
with this next time.

01:12:43.630 --> 01:12:45.190 align:middle line:84%
You'll turn in your
papers by email,

01:12:45.190 --> 01:12:48.000 align:middle line:84%
and we'll talk a little bit
more about intelligent tutors

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:50.615 align:middle line:84%
as well, both on Wednesday
and the following Monday.

01:12:50.615 --> 01:12:52.990 align:middle line:84%
If I can help you at all with
paper one or anything else,

01:12:52.990 --> 01:12:53.490 align:middle line:90%
let me know.

01:12:53.490 --> 01:12:56.390 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, have a wonderful
rest of your week.

01:12:56.390 --> 01:13:08.000 align:middle line:90%